var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"title":"SatelliteGridding.jl","page":"Home","location":"#SatelliteGridding.jl","category":"section","text":"A Julia package for gridding satellite Level-2 data onto rectangular, cubed-sphere, and HEALPix grids with footprint-aware oversampling."},{"title":"Overview","page":"Home","location":"#Overview","category":"section","text":"Satellite instruments observe through irregularly shaped footprints — typically quadrilaterals defined by 4 corner coordinates or circular footprints defined by bounds or center-plus-radius metadata. These footprints can overlap multiple output grid cells. SatelliteGridding.jl handles this by subdividing each footprint into n×n sub-pixels and using \"point-in-box\" binning to distribute observations across the grid."},{"title":"Key Features","page":"Home","location":"#Key-Features","category":"section","text":"Footprint oversampling: Subdivide quadrilateral or circular footprints into n×n sub-pixels for proper spatial overlap handling\nTOML configuration: Declarative data source definitions with filter expressions\nDate-based file discovery: Automatic file matching using YYYY/MM/DD/DOY placeholders\nQuality filtering: Flexible filter syntax (\"< 80\", \"> 0.5\", \"== 1\", \"1600 < x < 2200\")\nMultiple backends: Sequential (Welford), KA CPU, CUDA, or Metal execution\nStandard deviation: Optional per-cell standard deviation via Welford's algorithm\nCenter-coordinate gridding: MODIS-style gridding where each pixel maps to one cell\nVegetation indices: Built-in EVI, NDVI, NIRv, NDWI computation for MODIS data"},{"title":"Supported Instruments","page":"Home","location":"#Supported-Instruments","category":"section","text":"TROPOMI: SIF, NO₂, CH₄, CO (S5P Level-2)\nOCO-2/3: SIF, XCO₂ (Level-2)\nGOSAT: SIF products with circular footprints\nMODIS: Surface reflectance (center-coordinate gridding)\nAny Level-2 product with corner, bounding, or center-plus-radius coordinates in NetCDF format"},{"title":"Prerequisites","page":"Home","location":"#Prerequisites","category":"section","text":"Julia ≥ 1.10 is required. If you don't have Julia installed:\n\nDownload from julialang.org/downloads\nFollow the platform-specific install instructions\nVerify with julia --version"},{"title":"Installation","page":"Home","location":"#Installation","category":"section","text":"From the Julia REPL:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/cfranken/SatelliteGridding.git\")\n\nThis installs the package and all dependencies automatically.\n\nFor development (editable local clone):\n\nPkg.develop(url=\"https://github.com/cfranken/SatelliteGridding.git\")\n\nSee Cubed-Sphere Grids and HEALPix Grids for global spherical output grids."},{"title":"Quick Start — Julia API","page":"Home","location":"#Quick-Start-—-Julia-API","category":"section","text":"using SatelliteGridding, Dates\n\n# 1. Load a TOML config that describes the satellite data source\nconfig = load_config(\"examples/tropomi_sif.toml\")\n\n# 2. Define the output grid (global, 0.5° resolution)\ngrid_spec = GridSpec(\n    lat_min = -90f0,  lat_max = 90f0,\n    lon_min = -180f0, lon_max = 180f0,\n    dlat = 0.5f0, dlon = 0.5f0\n)\n\n# 3. Define the time range (8-day composites)\ntime_spec = TimeSpec(\n    DateTime(\"2019-01-01\"),\n    DateTime(\"2019-12-31\"),\n    Dates.Day(8)\n)\n\n# 4. Run the gridding\ngrid_l2(config, grid_spec, time_spec;\n        outfile = \"tropomi_sif_2019.nc\")"},{"title":"Reading the output","page":"Home","location":"#Reading-the-output","category":"section","text":"using NCDatasets\n\nds = Dataset(\"tropomi_sif_2019.nc\")\nsif     = ds[\"sif_743\"][:, :, 1]   # gridded SIF, first time step\nweights = ds[\"n\"][:, :, 1]          # observation count per cell\nclose(ds)"},{"title":"Key options for grid_l2","page":"Home","location":"#Key-options-for-[grid_l2](@ref)","category":"section","text":"Keyword Default Description\nn_oversample nothing (auto) Sub-pixel factor. nothing = auto-compute from footprint/grid ratio\ncompute_std false Compute per-cell standard deviation\noutfile \"gridded_output.nc\" Output NetCDF file path\nbackend nothing (sequential) CPU(), CUDABackend(), MetalBackend(), or another compatible KernelAbstractions backend\naccumulator_precision Backend default KA weights and daily moments. Float64 on KA CPU and CUDA, Float32 on Metal."},{"title":"Quick Start — Command Line","page":"Home","location":"#Quick-Start-—-Command-Line","category":"section","text":"The CLI has two commands: l2 (footprint oversampling) and center (MODIS-style).\n\n# TROPOMI SIF — 8-day composites at 0.5°\njulia --project=. bin/grid.jl l2 \\\n    --config SatelliteGridding/examples/tropomi_sif.toml \\\n    --dLat 0.5 --dLon 0.5 --dDays 8 \\\n    --startDate 2019-01-01 --stopDate 2019-12-31 \\\n    -o tropomi_sif_2019.nc\n\n# OCO-2 XCO₂ — monthly composites at 2°\njulia --project=. bin/grid.jl l2 \\\n    --config SatelliteGridding/examples/oco2_xco2.toml \\\n    --dLat 2.0 --dLon 2.0 --monthly --dDays 1 \\\n    --startDate 2015-01-01 --stopDate 2020-12-31 \\\n    -o oco2_xco2_monthly.nc\n\n# With parallel KA CPU backend\njulia --project=. bin/grid.jl l2 \\\n    --config SatelliteGridding/examples/tropomi_no2.toml \\\n    --backend cpu --dLat 0.25 --dLon 0.25 \\\n    -o tropomi_no2.nc\n\n# GOSAT SIF circular footprints\njulia --project=. bin/grid.jl l2 \\\n    --config SatelliteGridding/examples/gosat_sif_center_radius.toml \\\n    --footprint circle --nOversample 80 \\\n    --backend cpu \\\n    -o gosat_sif.nc\n\n# MODIS MCD43A4 with generated per-tile sinusoidal geolocation\njulia --project=. bin/grid.jl center \\\n    --config SatelliteGridding/examples/modis_reflectance.toml \\\n    --geoProvider modis --dLat 0.05 --dLon 0.05 \\\n    --startDate 2019-01-01 --stopDate 2019-12-31 \\\n    --vegIndices \\\n    -o modis_2019.nc\n\nSee the CLI Reference for all options and the GPU Acceleration page for CUDA setup."},{"title":"Backends","page":"Home","location":"#Backends","category":"section","text":"Backend CLI flag Julia API Description\nSequential --backend sequential default (backend=nothing) Welford's online mean. Supports --compSTD.\nKA CPU --backend cpu backend=CPU() Parallel sort + subpixels. Sum-based accumulation.\nKA CUDA --backend cuda backend=resolve_backend(\"cuda\") or CUDABackend() NVIDIA GPU pipeline. Requires CUDA.jl.\nKA Metal --backend metal backend=resolve_backend(\"metal\") or MetalBackend() Apple GPU pipeline. Requires Metal.jl on macOS/Apple GPU."},{"title":"Next Steps","page":"Home","location":"#Next-Steps","category":"section","text":"Tutorial: Step-by-step walkthrough with TROPOMI SIF data\nConfiguration: TOML config reference and filter expressions\nAlgorithm: How footprint oversampling works under the hood\nCLI Reference: All command-line flags\nGPU Acceleration: CUDA setup and performance guide\nAPI Reference: Full function documentation"},{"title":"API Reference","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#API-Reference","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Module","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Module","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Main Functions","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Main-Functions","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Backends","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Backends","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Types","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Types","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Cubed-Sphere Grids","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Cubed-Sphere-Grids","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"HEALPix Grids","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#HEALPix-Grids","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Oversampling","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Oversampling","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Geometry","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Geometry","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Accumulation","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Accumulation","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"I/O","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#I/O","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Geolocation","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Geolocation","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Filters","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Filters","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Vegetation Indices","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Vegetation-Indices","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"CLI","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CLI","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"SatelliteGridding","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding","category":"module","text":"SatelliteGridding\n\nA Julia package for gridding satellite Level-2 data onto regular rectangular grids with footprint-aware oversampling.\n\nSatellite instruments have irregularly shaped footprints (quadrilaterals defined by 4 corner coordinates) that can overlap multiple output grid cells. Rather than computing exact geometric intersections, this package subdivides each footprint into n×n sub-pixels and uses \"point-in-box\" binning. The mean per grid cell is computed incrementally using Welford's online algorithm for numerical stability.\n\nMain functions\n\ngrid_l2: Grid Level-2 data with footprint oversampling\ngrid_center: Grid data using center coordinates only (e.g., MODIS)\nload_config: Load a TOML/JSON data source configuration\n\nExample\n\nusing SatelliteGridding, Dates\n\nconfig = load_config(\"tropomi_sif.toml\")\ngrid_spec = GridSpec(lat_min=-90f0, lat_max=90f0, lon_min=-180f0, lon_max=180f0,\n                     dlat=1f0, dlon=1f0)\ntime_spec = TimeSpec(DateTime(\"2019-01-01\"), DateTime(\"2019-12-31\"),\n                     Day(8); oversample_temporal=1.0f0)\ngrid_l2(config, grid_spec, time_spec; outfile=\"output.nc\")\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.grid","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.grid","category":"function","text":"grid(config, grid_spec, time_spec; method=SubpixelGridding(), kwargs...)\ngrid(config, grid_spec, time_spec, method::AbstractGriddingMethod; kwargs...)\n\nDispatch-based library entry point for gridding. The CLI still exposes explicit l2 and center commands, while library callers can choose a gridding method with a first-class type such as SubpixelGridding, CircularFootprintGridding, or CenterPointGridding. Pass verbose=false to suppress status messages.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.grid_l2","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.grid_l2","category":"function","text":"grid_l2(config, grid_spec, time_spec; kwargs...)\n\nGrid Level-2 satellite data with footprint-aware oversampling.\n\nReads input NetCDF files as specified by config, applies quality filters, subdivides each satellite footprint into sub-pixels for proper overlap handling, and accumulates gridded averages. Output is written to a NetCDF4 file. Quadrilateral footprints are used by default; pass footprint_method=CircularFootprintGridding(...) for circular products such as GOSAT. Circular footprints can be read from lat_bnd/lon_bnd or from center lat/lon plus radius.\n\nTo archive Float64 results exactly, use Float64 accumulators and set [output] precision = \"float64\", significant_digits_mean = \"none\", and significant_digits_std = \"none\", with fill_value = nan. For tiled sequential runs, use Float64 bounds and spacings for GridSpec, or T=Float64 for CubedSphereGridSpec and HEALPixGridSpec. KA CPU and CUDA accumulate in Float64 by default, as do sequential rolling runs. On the sequential CLI use --accumulator-precision float64 to construct a Float64 grid. A CubedSphereGridSpec with T=Float32 computes cell centre and corner coordinates in Float32 before widening them to Float64 for NetCDF. Set T=Float64 to compute and write those coordinates in double precision. Use this configuration for aggregation inputs such as HEALPix 4:1 coarsening or regridding. At fine nside, quantization of nearly equal child means can dominate a small parent standard deviation.\n\nArguments\n\nconfig::DataSourceConfig: Data source configuration (from TOML/JSON)\ngrid_spec::AbstractGridSpec: Output grid geometry\ntime_spec::TimeSpec: Temporal binning parameters\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nn_oversample=nothing: Sub-pixel factor in 1:1024. nothing uses the grid's default.\nfootprint_method::AbstractGriddingMethod=SubpixelGridding(): Footprint geometry method.\ncompute_std::Bool=false: Also compute per-cell standard deviation on sequential and KA paths. KA replays input files to refine the mean for Float32 accumulators and whenever standard deviation is requested. Mean-only Float64 KA runs use a single pass.\nverbose::Bool=true: Log run status with @info.\noutfile::String=\"gridded_output.nc\": Output file path\nbackend: KernelAbstractions backend (default: nothing for sequential Welford path). Use CPU(), CUDABackend(), MetalBackend(), or another compatible KA backend.\ngeometry_precision=nothing: Spherical KA subdivision and locator precision. Use Float32 or Float64. Defaults to Float64 on CUDA and KA CPU and Float32 on Metal.\naccumulator_precision=nothing: KA precision for weights, sums, and daily moments. All rolling merges use Float64. Defaults to Float64 on CUDA and KA CPU and Float32 on Metal. With Float32, a standard deviation below roughly one ulp of the cell mean (σ/|mean| ≲ 1e-7) can be reported as zero. Use Float64 when such small relative variability matters.\nkeep_going::Bool=false: Continue after per-file errors. By default, the first file error stops the run.\nprogress::Bool=stderr isa Base.TTY: Draw progress bars (on stderr). By default they're drawn only when stderr is a terminal: redirected to a file or a pipe, as in batch jobs, every redraw would become new lines.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.grid_center","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.grid_center","category":"function","text":"grid_center(config, grid_spec, time_spec; kwargs...)\n\nGrid data using center coordinates only (no footprint bounds), suitable for MODIS-style data where each pixel maps to exactly one rectangular grid cell.\n\nTo archive Float64 center results exactly, construct GridSpec with Float64 bounds and spacings for Float64 accumulation and set [output] precision = \"float64\" and significant_digits_center = \"none\". The center CLI uses a Float32 grid. A cell below min_count can have n > 0 alongside missing science values.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\ngeo_table::Union{Nothing,String}=nothing: Path to a legacy monolithic geolocation lookup table (NetCDF)\ngeo_cache::Union{Nothing,String}=nothing: Directory for generated per-tile MODIS geolocation cache\ngeo_provider::Union{Symbol,String}=:auto: :auto, :variables, :lut, or :modis\nveg_indices::Bool=false: Compute vegetation indices (EVI, NDVI, NIRv, NDWI)\nverbose::Bool=true: Log run status with @info.\noutfile::String=\"gridded_output.nc\": Output file path\nkeep_going::Bool=false: Continue after per-file errors. By default, the first file error stops the run.\nprogress::Bool=stderr isa Base.TTY: Draw progress bars (on stderr). By default they're drawn only when stderr is a terminal: redirected to a file or a pipe, as in batch jobs, every redraw would become new lines.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.load_config","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.load_config","category":"function","text":"load_config(config_path::String) -> DataSourceConfig\n\nLoad a configuration file (TOML or JSON) and return a DataSourceConfig.\n\nThe file must contain a [grid] section plus filePattern and folder. Footprint-aware L2 gridding usually uses [basic] entries for lat_bnd and lon_bnd. Circular footprints can alternatively use center lat/lon plus a radius variable or scalar [circle] radius. Center-coordinate gridding can use [basic] lat and lon, a geolocation lookup table, or generated MODIS sinusoidal geolocation. Filters are defined in an optional [filter] section using intuitive string expressions:\n\nExample TOML\n\nfilePattern = \"S5P_PAL__L2B_SIF____YYYYMMDD*.nc\"\nfolder = \"/path/to/data/\"\n\n[basic]\nlat = \"PRODUCT/latitude\"\nlon = \"PRODUCT/longitude\"\nlat_bnd = \"PRODUCT/SUPPORT_DATA/GEOLOCATIONS/latitude_bounds\"\nlon_bnd = \"PRODUCT/SUPPORT_DATA/GEOLOCATIONS/longitude_bounds\"\n\n[grid]\nsif_743 = \"PRODUCT/SIF_743\"\nsif_735 = \"PRODUCT/SIF_735\"\n\n[filter]\n\"PRODUCT/SUPPORT_DATA/GEOLOCATIONS/solar_zenith_angle\" = \"< 80\"\n\"PRODUCT/qa_value\" = \"> 0.5\"\n\"PRODUCT/methane\" = \"1600 < x < 2200\"\n\"PRODUCT/mode\" = \"== 1\"\n\nThe legacy JSON format with separate filter_gt/filter_lt/filter_eq sections is still supported for backward compatibility.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.supported_backend_names","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.supported_backend_names","category":"function","text":"supported_backend_names() -> Tuple\n\nReturn the backend names accepted by resolve_backend and the CLI. sequential uses the single-threaded Welford path; the others use the KernelAbstractions accumulation path.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.backend_help_lines","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.backend_help_lines","category":"function","text":"backend_help_lines() -> Vector{String}\n\nShort descriptions for CLI help and documentation.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.resolve_backend","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.resolve_backend","category":"function","text":"resolve_backend(name; grid_spec=nothing, geometry_precision=nothing, accumulator_precision=nothing) -> Union{Nothing, KernelAbstractions.Backend}\n\nResolve a user-facing backend name to a KernelAbstractions backend object. The optional GPU packages are weak dependencies and are loaded only when the corresponding backend is requested. Pass grid_spec to check that it supports the backend before loading any optional packages. Pass explicit KA precisions here to validate them before loading a backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.cuda_backend","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.cuda_backend","category":"function","text":"cuda_backend()\n\nReturn a CUDA KernelAbstractions backend after CUDA.jl has been loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.metal_backend","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.metal_backend","category":"function","text":"metal_backend()\n\nReturn a Metal KernelAbstractions backend after Metal.jl has been loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.AbstractGridSpec","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.AbstractGridSpec","category":"type","text":"AbstractGridSpec{T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nCommon supertype for output-grid specifications. Subtypes describe how a satellite observation's (lat, lon) maps onto cell indices on a particular grid geometry and how the matching output NetCDF file is laid out.\n\nConcrete subtypes G <: AbstractGridSpec{T} supply their geometry to the same gridder, oversampling, and NetCDF machinery via multiple dispatch.\n\nMethod Returns Purpose\nto_fractional_index(spec, lat, lon) Tuple of fractional indices Project a point onto continuous cell indices where the geometry supports them\nin_bounds(spec, lat, lon) Bool Cheap scalar bounding-box pre-filter\ngrid_shape(spec) NTuple{N, Int} Spatial grid size in index order, used by default for the accumulator\npayload_dims(spec) NTuple{N, String} NetCDF dimension names for payload variables\nwrite_coordinates!(ds, spec) Nothing Write the spatial coordinate axes / aux coords to a NetCDF dataset\n\nFor grids with fractional indices, flooring the components gives one-based integer indices in grid_shape(spec) axis order. The return type is Tuple because an axis with categorical indices may use a different type. The spatial dimensionality N of the accumulator is 2 for regular lat/lon grids ((n_lon, n_lat)) and for cubed spheres (which fold (Nc, Nc, 6) into (Nc, 6·Nc)).\n\nWhole-sphere grids locate subpoints directly through the AbstractSphericalGridSpec interface. Their on-disk dimensions can differ from the folded accumulator shape.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.AbstractSphericalGridSpec","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.AbstractSphericalGridSpec","category":"type","text":"AbstractSphericalGridSpec{T} <: AbstractGridSpec{T}\n\nWhole-sphere grid for quadrilateral footprint gridding. Implement these methods to use a new spherical grid with grid.\n\n_device_locator(::MyGrid) returns an isbits locator. Define _device_locator(::MyGrid, ::Type{F}) where {F} instead when the locator stores geometry of type F.\nlocate(locator, x, y, z) returns the one-based linear accumulator index of a unit vector using allocation-free device operations.\ngrid_shape(spec) returns the two-dimensional accumulator shape, or implement accumulator_shape(spec) directly to specify a folded layout.\n_output_spatial_shape(spec) returns the NetCDF spatial shape in Julia dimension order. The writer reshapes the accumulator in linear index order.\npayload_dims(spec) returns the corresponding NetCDF spatial dimension names.\nwrite_coordinates!(ds, spec) defines those dimensions and writes coordinates.\n\nSequential and KernelAbstractions gridding both subdivide footprints in unit-vector space. Sequential gridding requests a Float64 locator. Optionally implement _device_cell_spacing(::MyLocator, ::Type{F}) where {F} to supply the angular grid spacing for adaptive subdivision. The default uses four samples per side.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.RectangularGridSpec","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.RectangularGridSpec","category":"type","text":"RectangularGridSpec{T<:AbstractFloat} <: AbstractGridSpec{T}\n\nOutput grid on a regular latitude/longitude rectangle with uniform cell spacing. The legacy name GridSpec is a const alias of this type for back-compatibility.\n\nFields\n\nlat_min, lat_max: Latitude bounds (degrees)\nlon_min, lon_max: Longitude bounds (degrees)\ndlat, dlon: Grid cell size (degrees)\nlat, lon: Vectors of cell center coordinates\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.GridSpec","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.GridSpec","category":"type","text":"Back-compat alias for RectangularGridSpec. All call sites using GridSpec(...) and GridSpec{T} keep working unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.TimeSpec","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.TimeSpec","category":"type","text":"TimeSpec\n\nSpecification of the temporal gridding parameters.\n\nTwo temporal modes are supported:\n\nTiled (default): snapshots are spaced by time_step; each snapshot averages a forward window [d, d + time_step·oversample_temporal − 1 day]. With oversample_temporal == 1 the windows tile with no overlap.\nRolling mean: set sample_step and window_halfwidth_days. Snapshots are spaced by sample_step and each averages a centered window [c − N, c + N] (N = window_halfwidth_days). When 2N+1 > sample_step the windows overlap, smoothing the temporal evolution. Each calendar day is gridded only once and reused across overlapping windows (in-memory day cache). time_step/oversample_temporal are ignored in this mode.\n\nFields\n\nstart_date, stop_date: Date range of snapshot centers\ntime_step: Tiled-mode bin size (Dates.Day or Dates.Month)\noversample_temporal: Tiled-mode window multiplier (>1 gives moving-average-like behavior)\nsample_step: Rolling-mode snapshot spacing (Dates.Day), or nothing for tiled mode\nwindow_halfwidth_days: Rolling-mode half-width N (days), or nothing for tiled mode\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.DataSourceConfig","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.DataSourceConfig","category":"type","text":"DataSourceConfig\n\nConfiguration loaded from a TOML/JSON file that defines a satellite data source.\n\nFields\n\nbasic: Maps internal keys (\"lat\", \"lon\", \"lat_bnd\", \"lon_bnd\", \"radius\") to variable paths in the NetCDF files\ngrid_vars: Ordered mapping of output variable names to input variable paths (all will be gridded)\nfilters: Quality filter rules parsed from [filter] section\nfile_pattern: Glob pattern with YYYY/MM/DD/DOY placeholders for finding input files\nfolder: Root folder for input data (may also contain YYYY/MM/DD placeholders)\noptions: Processing settings plus nested [output] storage and [metadata] selection tables\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.FilterRule","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.FilterRule","category":"type","text":"FilterRule\n\nA single filter condition for a NetCDF variable.\n\nFields\n\nvariable: Path to the NetCDF variable (may include group separators /)\nop: Operation — :lt, :gt, :eq, :between\nlo: Lower bound (threshold for :gt/:lt/:eq, low end for :between)\nhi: Upper bound (only used for :between)\n\nConfig syntax (TOML [filter] section)\n\n[filter]\n\"solar_zenith_angle\" = \"< 80\"         # less than\n\"qa_value\" = \"> 0.5\"                  # greater than\n\"mode\" = \"== 1\"                       # equality\n\"methane\" = \"1600 < x < 2200\"         # range (between)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.AbstractGriddingMethod","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.AbstractGriddingMethod","category":"type","text":"AbstractGriddingMethod\n\nDispatch marker for choosing how observations are mapped onto the output grid. Use SubpixelGridding for footprint-aware oversampling, CircularFootprintGridding for circular or near-circular footprints, CenterPointGridding for fast center-coordinate binning, and ExactIntersectionGridding as the future exact geometry hook.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.SubpixelGridding","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.SubpixelGridding","category":"type","text":"SubpixelGridding(; n_oversample=nothing)\n\nFootprint-aware gridding by sampling an n × n grid of subpoints inside each quadrilateral footprint. Set n_oversample to an integer in 1:1024 to use a fixed sample count, or leave it as nothing to use the grid's default.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.CircularFootprintGridding","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.CircularFootprintGridding","category":"type","text":"CircularFootprintGridding(; n_oversample=nothing)\n\nFootprint-aware gridding for circular or near-circular footprints described by a center coordinate and four bounding coordinates (or a radius). Symmetric latitude and longitude radii are inferred from the farthest corner (max |corner - center| along each axis; ≲ 0.1% edge weight bias for GOSAT-like viewing geometry), and an n × n grid over the bounding box is masked to the inferred circle or ellipse. Out-of-grid sub-samples are dropped without renormalizing remaining weights. An explicit count must be in 1:1024; nothing keeps the automatic oversampling heuristic.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.CenterPointGridding","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.CenterPointGridding","category":"type","text":"CenterPointGridding()\n\nFast center-coordinate gridding. Each observation or pixel contributes to the single output cell containing its center coordinate.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.ExactIntersectionGridding","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.ExactIntersectionGridding","category":"type","text":"ExactIntersectionGridding()\n\nReserved method for exact footprint/grid-cell intersection gridding. This is not implemented yet, but the type keeps the public API open for an exact geometry backend without changing callers.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.CubedSphereGridSpec","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.CubedSphereGridSpec","category":"type","text":"CubedSphereGridSpec{T,D} <: AbstractSphericalGridSpec{T}\n\nCubed-sphere output grid with Nc cells per panel edge and 6 panels (6·Nc² cells total). The default definition is GMAOCubedSphereDefinition — the GEOS/GCHP convention (equal-distance gnomonic, GEOS-native panels, -10° longitude shift), e.g. CubedSphereGridSpec(Nc=360) for C360.\n\nAccumulator folding\n\nInternally the cube (Nc, Nc, 6) is folded into a 2D accumulator of shape grid_shape(spec) = (Nc, 6·Nc) so the existing rectangular gridder machinery (_welford_update!, finalize_mean!, accumulate_center!) is reused unchanged: cell (i, j, panel) maps to col = i, row = (panel-1)·Nc + j. to_fractional_index returns the folded (col, row); the NetCDF writer un-folds via reshape(slice, Nc, Nc, 6) back to (Xdim, Ydim, nf).\n\nFields\n\nNc::Int: cells per panel edge\ndefinition::D: cubed-sphere geometry contract\ncenters_lon, centers_lat: (Nc, Nc, 6) cell-center coords (degrees, lon in [0,360))\ncorners_lon, corners_lat: (Nc+1, Nc+1, 6) cell-corner coords (degrees)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.CubedSphereDefinition","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.CubedSphereDefinition","category":"type","text":"CubedSphereDefinition(coordinate_law, center_law, panel_convention;\n                      longitude_offset_deg=0, tag=:custom)\n\nComplete horizontal cubed-sphere geometry contract: how logical face edges are placed on the gnomonic cube (coordinate_law), how cell centers are derived (center_law), how the six faces are ordered/oriented (panel_convention), and a final rigid z-axis rotation (longitude_offset_deg). For native GMAO files use GMAOCubedSphereDefinition.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.GMAOCubedSphereDefinition","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.GMAOCubedSphereDefinition","category":"function","text":"GMAOCubedSphereDefinition(; convention=GEOSNativePanelConvention(),\n                            longitude_offset_deg=-10)\n\nNative GMAO/GEOS cubed-sphere definition used by GEOS-IT C180 / GEOS-FP C720 (and GCHP when driven by GMAO met): GMAOEqualDistanceGnomonic edges, FourCornerNormalizedCenter centers, GEOSNativePanelConvention, and a final -10° longitude shift. This is the default for CubedSphereGridSpec.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.EquiangularCubedSphereDefinition","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.EquiangularCubedSphereDefinition","category":"function","text":"EquiangularCubedSphereDefinition(; convention=GnomonicPanelConvention(),\n                                   longitude_offset_deg=0)\n\nLegacy synthetic/equiangular definition: EquiangularGnomonic corners plus AngularMidpointCenter centers.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.lonlat_to_panel_xy","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.lonlat_to_panel_xy","category":"function","text":"lonlat_to_panel_xy(def::CubedSphereDefinition, Nc, lon_deg, lat_deg, FT)\n    -> (panel::Int, s_frac, t_frac)\n\nClosed-form inverse of the cubed-sphere forward map for a single (lon, lat) in degrees. Returns the panel id panel ∈ 1..6 and continuous edge indices s_frac, t_frac on the panel's [1, Nc+1)² parameter square (half-open at the upper edge). The integer cell index is floor(Int, s_frac) (x) and floor(Int, t_frac) (y), guaranteed in 1..Nc for every finite (lon, lat) — upper-edge points are nudged to prevfloat(Nc+1) and sub-1 round-off is raised to 1, the standard half-open-cell convention. The whole sphere is covered.\n\nHonors the definition's panel convention, coordinate law, and longitude offset.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.grid_shape","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.grid_shape","category":"function","text":"grid_shape(spec::AbstractGridSpec) -> NTuple{N, Int}\n\nSpatial grid dimensions in index order. For grids with to_fractional_index, the axis order matches the fractional indices. The gridder allocates this shape by default, and a grid may specialize accumulator_shape separately.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.accumulator_shape","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.accumulator_shape","category":"function","text":"accumulator_shape(spec::AbstractGridSpec) -> NTuple{M, Int}\n\nSpatial dimensions of the in-memory accumulator array that the gridder allocates, in the same axis order as the indices returned by to_fractional_index or the spherical unit-vector locator. The Welford / scatter machinery indexes this layout with (col, row).\n\nThe default is grid_shape. A cubed-sphere grid folds its (Nc, Nc, 6) cube into a 2D (Nc, 6·Nc) accumulator and unfolds only at write time. HEALPix folds the NESTED sequence into (nside^2, 12) in memory and writes it on a single cell axis. Both use the default because their grid_shape is already folded.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.HEALPixGridSpec","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.HEALPixGridSpec","category":"type","text":"HEALPixGridSpec(; nside, T=Float32, cell_bounds=false)\n\nWhole-sphere HEALPix grid in NESTED order with equal solid angle per cell. The resolution is nside = 2^level. Accumulators fold the cells into (nside^2, 12) so linear index pix + 1 addresses NESTED cell pix. Tiled sequential accumulators use T, and sequential rolling accumulators use Float64. Select KA geometry and accumulator precision with grid_l2. Float32 accumulators (used on Metal) can report standard deviations below roughly one ulp of the cell mean (σ/|mean| ≲ 1e-7) as zero. Use Float64 accumulators (the CUDA and KA CPU default) for small relative variability. Float32 KA runs replay input files to refine the cell means. Float64 KA runs replay input files when standard deviation is requested. Sequential projections and output coordinates use Float64. Cell centres and optional vertex bounds are computed when writing NetCDF.\n\nTo archive Float64 results exactly, use T=Float64 for tiled sequential gridding or Float64 KA accumulators, and set [output] precision = \"float64\", significant_digits_mean = \"none\", significant_digits_std = \"none\", and fill_value = nan. On the sequential CLI, pass --accumulator-precision float64. Both --backend cpu and --backend cuda accumulate in Float64 by default. Use this configuration for HEALPix 4:1 coarsening and regridding because quantization of nearly equal child means can dominate a small parent standard deviation at fine nside.\n\nThe grid uses input geodetic latitude directly as spherical latitude, consistent with the cubed sphere. True WGS84 cell areas vary by about 1.35% from equator to pole (−0.45% at the equator and +0.90% at the poles relative to the nominal spherical cell_area).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.ang2pix_nest","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.ang2pix_nest","category":"function","text":"ang2pix_nest(nside, lon_deg, lat_deg) -> Int64\n\nLocate a point on a HEALPix grid using the zero-based NESTED convention of Górski et al. (2005, ApJ 622, 759). Longitude is periodic, with edge ties evaluated on the canonical branch [0, 360) degrees. Latitude is spherical, in degrees within [-90, 90].\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.vec2pix_nest","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.vec2pix_nest","category":"function","text":"vec2pix_nest(nside, x, y, z) -> Int64\n\nLocate a finite unit vector in zero-based HEALPix NESTED order. The components share a precision of Float32 or Float64. At an exact pole with zero transverse components the vector convention uses longitude zero. Use Float64 for very fine grids. Above roughly nside = 2^21, Float32 pixel errors can exceed one cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.pix2ang_nest","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.pix2ang_nest","category":"function","text":"pix2ang_nest(nside, pix) -> (lon_deg, lat_deg)\n\nReturn the spherical coordinates in degrees of the centre of zero-based NESTED pixel pix. Longitude is in [0, 360).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.healpix_corners","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.healpix_corners","category":"function","text":"healpix_corners(nside, pix) -> (lons, lats)\n\nReturn the four vertex longitudes and latitudes in degrees for zero-based NESTED pixel pix, as tuples in north, west, south, east order, matching healpy.boundaries(step=1). Longitude is in [0, 360).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.nside2npix","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.nside2npix","category":"function","text":"nside2npix(nside) -> Int64\n\nReturn the number of HEALPix cells, 12*nside^2, for a NESTED grid. nside must be a power of two between 1 and 2^29.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.healpix_parent","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.healpix_parent","category":"function","text":"healpix_parent(pix) -> Int64\n\nReturn the zero-based NESTED index one level above pix. The given pixel must belong to a level above the base resolution (nside > 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.sort_corners_ccw","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.sort_corners_ccw","category":"function","text":"sort_corners_ccw(lat_corners, lon_corners) -> (sorted_lat, sorted_lon)\n\nSort 4 footprint corners into counter-clockwise order by computing the angle from the centroid to each corner. This ensures edges 1→2 and 4→3 are always opposite edges, which is required by compute_subpixels!.\n\nDifferent satellite products store corners in different orders (e.g., TROPOMI uses CCW order while OCO-2 uses a different convention). This function normalizes them.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.sort_corners_ccw!","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.sort_corners_ccw!","category":"function","text":"sort_corners_ccw!(lat_mat, lon_mat)\n\nSort corners in-place for all soundings in N×4 matrices.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.sort_corners_ccw_ka!","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.sort_corners_ccw_ka!","category":"function","text":"sort_corners_ccw_ka!(backend, lat_mat, lon_mat)\n\nKA kernel that sorts footprint corners into CCW order for all soundings in parallel. Uses a 5-comparator sorting network (optimal for 4 elements, no allocations).\n\nlat_mat, lon_mat: N×4 matrices of corner coordinates\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.compute_subpixels!","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.compute_subpixels!","category":"function","text":"compute_subpixels!(points, vert_lat, vert_lon, n, lats_0, lons_0, lats_1, lons_1)\n\nFill the n×n×2 array points with sub-pixel positions inside the quadrilateral defined by 4 corner coordinates (vert_lat[1:4], vert_lon[1:4]).\n\nThe algorithm:\n\nSubdivide edge 1→2 into n baseline points\nSubdivide edge 4→3 into n baseline points\nFor each pair of corresponding baseline points, subdivide the connecting line into n interior points\n\nThis produces n² sub-pixels that fill the footprint quadrilateral. Temporary buffers lats_0, lons_0, lats_1, lons_1 (each length n) must be provided.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.floor_indices!","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.floor_indices!","category":"function","text":"floor_indices!(ix, iy, points)\n\nConvert the floating-point sub-pixel positions in points to integer grid cell indices via floor. Results stored in ix and iy (each length n²).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.compute_n_oversample","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.compute_n_oversample","category":"function","text":"compute_n_oversample(footprint_extent, cell_size; n_min=2, n_max=20)\n\nEstimate an appropriate oversampling factor n based on the ratio of footprint size to grid cell size. Aims for ~3 sub-pixels per grid cell width.\n\nReturns an integer in [n_min, n_max].\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.compute_footprint_indices_ka!","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.compute_footprint_indices_ka!","category":"function","text":"compute_footprint_indices_ka!(backend, ix_out, iy_out, skip_flag,\n                              lat_corners, lon_corners, n;\n                              n_lon=typemax(Int32), n_lat=typemax(Int32))\n\nCompute n×n sub-pixel grid indices for each quadrilateral footprint on backend, mirroring the sequential compute_subpixels! and floor_indices! arithmetic.\n\nOn backends that support double precision (KA CPU, CUDA), corners remain in their input element type, edge subdivisions are converted to Float64 baseline coordinates, connecting-line subdivisions are evaluated in Float64, and sub-sample coordinates are rounded to the point storage type (matching eltype(lat_corners) here, or the grid accumulator type T when invoked from accumulate_batch!) before floor. On Metal, where Float64 is unavailable, all steps execute in Float32 and boundary cell assignments may differ from the sequential path.\n\nSets skip_flag[fp] = 2 if the footprint's longitude extent >= n (too wide to oversample meaningfully) or if a single-cell fast-path footprint falls outside 1..n_lon × 1..n_lat. For single-cell fast-path footprints inside the grid, stores the cell index in position 1 and marks all other positions -1.\n\nSub-pixels outside 1..n_lon × 1..n_lat are marked -1 and dropped without renormalizing remaining sub-pixel weights (1/n²), keeping boundary-cell weights equal to true fractional footprint overlap so regional boundary means remain domain-invariant. On global rectangular grids, samples beyond ±180° longitude or beyond the poles are dropped rather than wrapped.\n\nix_out: (n_fp, n²) Int32 — lon grid cell indices (-1 for masked/out-of-bounds)\niy_out: (n_fp, n²) Int32 — lat grid cell indices (-1 for masked/out-of-bounds)\nskip_flag: (n_fp,) Int32 — 0=oversample, 1=fast-path (single cell), 2=skip (too wide or out of bounds)\nlat_corners, lon_corners: (n_fp, 4) — fractional grid indices of corners\nn: oversampling factor\nn_lon, n_lat: grid dimensions for bounds clipping (default: typemax(Int32))\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.compute_circular_footprint_indices_ka!","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.compute_circular_footprint_indices_ka!","category":"function","text":"compute_circular_footprint_indices_ka!(backend, ix_out, iy_out, inside_count,\n                                       skip_flag, center_lat, center_lon,\n                                       lat_corners, lon_corners, n;\n                                       n_lon=typemax(Int32), n_lat=typemax(Int32))\n\nCompute sampled grid-cell indices for circular or near-circular footprints on backend, mirroring accumulate_circular_footprint!. Symmetric latitude and longitude radii are inferred from the farthest corner (max |corner - center| along each axis; ≲ 0.1% edge weight bias for GOSAT-like viewing geometry). On backends that support double precision (KA CPU, CUDA), centers and corners are promoted internally to Float64 for radius calculation and sample-loop evaluation (matching _circle_footprint_axes and accumulate_circular_footprint!); on Metal, where Float64 is unavailable, these steps execute in Float32 and boundary cell assignments may differ from the sequential path.\n\nPoints outside the inferred circle/ellipse or outside 1..n_lon × 1..n_lat are marked -1; inside_count[fp] stores all samples inside the circle/ellipse so out-of-grid samples are dropped without renormalizing in-grid weights, keeping boundary-cell weights equal to true fractional footprint overlap and regional boundary means domain-invariant. On global rectangular grids, samples beyond ±180° longitude or beyond the poles are dropped rather than wrapped.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.polygon_area","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.polygon_area","category":"function","text":"polygon_area(points)\n\nCompute planar polygon area with the shoelace formula. Points are (x, y) tuples in grid-index coordinates.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.exact_footprint_weights","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.exact_footprint_weights","category":"function","text":"exact_footprint_weights(lat_corners, lon_corners; n_lon, n_lat)\n\nCompute exact planar overlap weights between one quadrilateral footprint and regular grid cells in fractional grid-index coordinates. The returned matrix has shape (n_lon, n_lat) and sums to one when the footprint is fully inside the domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.circle_footprint_weights","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.circle_footprint_weights","category":"function","text":"circle_footprint_weights(center_lat, center_lon, lat_corners, lon_corners;\n                         n_lon, n_lat, n_oversample)\n\nCompute sampled per-cell weights for one circular or near-circular footprint in fractional grid-index coordinates. Symmetric latitude and longitude radii are inferred from the farthest corner (max |corner - center| along each axis; ≲ 0.1% edge weight bias for GOSAT-like viewing geometry). In grid-index space the sampled shape may be an ellipse when the output grid has unequal latitude and longitude resolutions. Sub-samples outside 1..n_lon × 1..n_lat are dropped without renormalizing remaining weights so boundary-cell weights reflect true fractional footprint overlap.\n\n\n\n\n\ncircle_footprint_weights(center_lat, center_lon, radius_lat, radius_lon;\n                         n_lon, n_lat, n_oversample)\n\nConvenience overload for a circular or elliptical footprint specified directly by center coordinate and radius in fractional grid-index units.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.footprint_weights","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.footprint_weights","category":"function","text":"footprint_weights(method, lat_corners, lon_corners; n_lon, n_lat)\n\nReturn per-cell footprint weights for a single footprint using the requested geometry method. This is a lightweight geometry-level API used by tests and by future exact-intersection gridders.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.accumulate_footprint!","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.accumulate_footprint!","category":"function","text":"accumulate_footprint!(grid_data, grid_std, grid_weights, compute_std,\n                      lat_idx, lon_idx, values, n_pixels, n_vars, n_oversample,\n                      points_buf, ix_buf, iy_buf,\n                      lats0_buf, lons0_buf, lats1_buf, lons1_buf)\n\nAccumulate a batch of satellite observations into the output grid using footprint-aware oversampling and Welford's online averaging algorithm.\n\nFor each pixel:\n\nFast path: If all 4 corner coordinates fall into the same grid cell, the full observation value is assigned to that cell (weight=1).\nOversample path: If the footprint spans multiple cells, it is subdivided into n_oversample × n_oversample sub-pixels. Each sub-pixel contributes weight 1/n² to whichever grid cell it falls in.\n\nSub-pixels (or single-cell footprints) that fall outside 1..n_lon × 1..n_lat are dropped without renormalizing remaining weights so boundary-cell weights reflect true fractional footprint overlap and remain domain-invariant. On global rectangular grids, samples beyond ±180° longitude or beyond the poles are dropped rather than wrapped.\n\nThe Welford algorithm maintains a running mean and sum-of-squared-deviations (M2), enabling numerically stable computation of mean and variance in a single pass.\n\nArguments\n\ngrid_data: Output array (n_lon, n_lat, n_vars) — running mean\ngrid_std: Output array (n_lon, n_lat, n_vars) — running M2 (sum of squared deviations); may be nothing when compute_std=false\ngrid_weights: Output array (n_lon, n_lat) — accumulated weights\ncompute_std: Whether to track variance\nlat_idx, lon_idx: Pre-computed fractional grid indices for corners, size (n_pixels, 4)\nvalues: Input data, size (n_pixels, n_vars)\nn_pixels: Number of observations in this batch\nn_vars: Number of variables being gridded\nn_oversample: Sub-pixel subdivision factor (n)\nRemaining arguments are pre-allocated work buffers\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.accumulate_footprint_cs!","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.accumulate_footprint_cs!","category":"function","text":"accumulate_footprint_cs!(grid_data, grid_std, grid_weights, compute_std, spec,\n                         lat_c, lon_c, values, n_pixels, n_vars, n_oversample,\n                         points_buf, lats0_buf, lons0_buf, lats1_buf, lons1_buf)\n\nDeprecated. Use grid_l2 with a CubedSphereGridSpec for footprint accumulation. This wrapper accepts unordered corners in degrees and uses unit-vector subdivision with the supplied n_oversample. The old longitude/latitude work buffers are ignored.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.accumulate_circular_footprint!","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.accumulate_circular_footprint!","category":"function","text":"accumulate_circular_footprint!(grid_data, grid_std, grid_weights, compute_std,\n                               center_lat_idx, center_lon_idx,\n                               lat_idx, lon_idx, values,\n                               n_pixels, n_vars, n_oversample)\n\nAccumulate observations with circular or near-circular footprints. Symmetric latitude and longitude radii are inferred from the farthest corner (max |corner - center| along each axis; ≲ 0.1% edge weight bias for GOSAT-like viewing geometry), and sampling is performed in Float64 fractional grid-index space.\n\nSub-samples outside 1..n_lon × 1..n_lat are dropped without renormalizing the per-cell weight hits / inside_count, keeping boundary-cell weights equal to true fractional footprint overlap so regional boundary means remain domain-invariant. On global rectangular grids, samples beyond ±180° longitude or beyond the poles are dropped rather than wrapped.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.accumulate_batch!","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.accumulate_batch!","category":"function","text":"accumulate_batch!(backend, grid_sum, grid_weights,\n                  lat_corners, lon_corners, values, n, n_vars)\n\nAccumulate quadrilateral observations into the grid using the KA kernel pipeline. This function works on KA CPU, CUDA, Metal, and other compatible backends.\n\nThe pipeline:\n\nSort corners into CCW order in the input coordinate type (or Float32 on Metal)\nCompute n×n subpixel grid indices per footprint, mirroring accumulate_footprint! (compute_subpixels! / floor_indices!, rounding sub-sample positions to the accumulator type T before floor on backends with Float64 support, and executing in Float32 on Metal)\nScatter-accumulate weighted sums into grid cells (using atomics on GPU)\n\nSub-pixels outside 1..n_lon × 1..n_lat are dropped without renormalizing remaining weights (1/n²) so boundary-cell weights reflect true fractional footprint overlap and regional boundary means remain domain-invariant. On global rectangular grids, samples beyond ±180° longitude or beyond the poles are dropped rather than wrapped.\n\nAfter processing all files, call finalize_mean! to convert sums to means.\n\nThis low-level helper accumulates weighted sums in grid_sum. Rectangular and spherical grid_l2 use a shared reference-shifted accumulator. They replay the inputs to refine Float32 means and to compute standard deviations when requested.\n\nArguments\n\nbackend: KernelAbstractions backend (CPU(), CUDABackend(), MetalBackend(), etc.)\ngrid_sum: (n_lon, n_lat, n_vars) — weighted sum accumulator\ngrid_weights: (n_lon, n_lat) — weight accumulator\nlat_corners, lon_corners: (n_fp, 4) — fractional grid indices of corners\nvalues: (n_fp, n_vars) — observation values\nn: oversampling factor\nn_vars: number of variables being gridded\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.accumulate_circular_batch!","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.accumulate_circular_batch!","category":"function","text":"accumulate_circular_batch!(backend, grid_sum, grid_weights,\n                           center_lat, center_lon,\n                           lat_corners, lon_corners, values, n, n_vars)\n\nAccumulate circular or near-circular footprints using the KA pipeline. This is the sum-based backend counterpart to accumulate_circular_footprint! and works with KA CPU, CUDA, Metal, and other compatible backends.\n\nSymmetric latitude and longitude radii are inferred from the farthest corner (max |corner - center| along each axis; ≲ 0.1% edge weight bias for GOSAT-like viewing geometry). On backends that support double precision (KA CPU, CUDA), radii and sub-sample positions are promoted to Float64 inside the index kernel to mirror accumulate_circular_footprint!; on Metal, where Float64 is unavailable, they execute in Float32 and boundary cell assignments may differ from the sequential path. Each footprint contributes weight k / inside_count to a cell with k hits. Sub-samples outside 1..n_lon × 1..n_lat are dropped without renormalization, so boundary-cell weights reflect true fractional footprint overlap and remain domain-invariant. On global rectangular grids, this also applies beyond ±180° longitude and beyond the poles.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.accumulate_center!","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.accumulate_center!","category":"function","text":"accumulate_center!(grid_data, grid_weights, lat, lon, values, grid_spec)\n\nAccumulate observations using center coordinates only (no footprint bounds). Used for MODIS-style gridding where each pixel maps to exactly one grid cell. Simple sum accumulation — divide by grid_weights afterward to get the mean.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.scatter_accumulate!","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.scatter_accumulate!","category":"function","text":"scatter_accumulate!(grid_sum, grid_weights, ix, iy, skip_flag, values, n, n_vars)\n\nSequential scatter-accumulate of weighted values into grid cells. Uses precomputed grid indices from compute_footprint_indices_ka!.\n\nFor each footprint:\n\nskip_flag == 1 (fast path): add weight=1, value to single cell\nskip_flag == 0 (oversample): add weight=1/n² to each subpixel's cell\nskip_flag == 2 (skip): do nothing\n\nSamples whose (col, row) falls outside 1..n_lon × 1..n_lat are dropped. This function is sequential (no atomics needed) and works with any array type. For GPU backends, use the @kernel version with @atomic instead.\n\ngrid_sum: (nlon, nlat, n_vars) — weighted sum accumulator\ngrid_weights: (nlon, nlat) — weight accumulator\nix, iy: (n_fp, n²) — precomputed grid indices\nskip_flag: (n_fp,) — 0=oversample, 1=fast-path, 2=skip\nvalues: (nfp, nvars) — observation values\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.scatter_accumulate_ka!","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.scatter_accumulate_ka!","category":"function","text":"scatter_accumulate_ka!(backend, grid_sum, grid_weights,\n                       ix, iy, skip_flag, values, n, n_vars)\n\nScatter in footprint order on CPU and use atomic updates on GPU. Samples outside 1..n_lon × 1..n_lat are dropped.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.scatter_accumulate_circular!","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.scatter_accumulate_circular!","category":"function","text":"scatter_accumulate_circular!(grid_sum, grid_weights, ix, iy, inside_count,\n                             skip_flag, values, n, n_vars)\n\nSequential scatter-accumulate for circular footprints. Oversampled footprints contribute once per cell, with weight k / inside_count[fp] for k hits. Samples outside 1..n_lon × 1..n_lat are dropped without renormalization.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.scatter_accumulate_circular_ka!","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.scatter_accumulate_circular_ka!","category":"function","text":"scatter_accumulate_circular_ka!(backend, grid_sum, grid_weights,\n                                ix, iy, inside_count, skip_flag,\n                                values, n, n_vars)\n\nScatter in footprint order on CPU and use atomic updates on GPU. Each footprint contributes weight k / inside_count[fp] to a cell with k hits. Out-of-grid samples are dropped without renormalization.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.finalize_mean!","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.finalize_mean!","category":"function","text":"finalize_mean!(grid_data, grid_weights)\n\nConvert weighted-sum accumulator to mean: mean = sum / weight. Cells with near-zero weight are left as zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.find_files","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.find_files","category":"function","text":"find_files(pattern::String, folder::String, date::DateTime) -> Vector{String}\n\nFind input files matching the given pattern for a specific date.\n\nResolves YYYY, YY, MM, DD, and DOY placeholders in both pattern and folder, then globs for matching files. Returns only non-empty files.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.read_nc_variable","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.read_nc_variable","category":"function","text":"read_nc_variable(dataset, path::String; bounds=false)\n\nRead a variable from a NetCDF dataset, handling nested groups via / separators.\n\nIf bounds=true, reshape the result so that footprint vertices are in the last dimension, returning an N×4 matrix (N soundings, 4 corners).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.read_nc_attribute","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.read_nc_attribute","category":"function","text":"read_nc_attribute(dataset, path::String, attr::String)\n\nRead an attribute from a possibly group-nested variable.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.read_variable_from_file","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.read_variable_from_file","category":"function","text":"read_variable_from_file(path, variable; bounds=false)\n\nRead a variable from a NetCDF-like file. Falls back to the system libnetcdf for flat HDF4/HDF-EOS variables when the Julia NetCDF build cannot open them.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.read_array_from_file","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.read_array_from_file","category":"function","text":"read_array_from_file(path, variable)\n\nRead a variable while preserving its native dimensionality.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.read_system_netcdf_variable","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.read_system_netcdf_variable","category":"function","text":"read_system_netcdf_variable(path, varname)\n\nRead a flat variable through the system libnetcdf. This is primarily a fallback for HDF4/HDF-EOS2 files when the Julia NetCDF_jll build lacks HDF4 support.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.create_output_dataset","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.create_output_dataset","category":"function","text":"create_output_dataset(outfile, grid_spec, n_times, grid_vars, compute_std) -> Dataset, Dict\n\nCreate the output NetCDF4 file with dimensions, coordinate variables, and data variable definitions. Returns the dataset and a Dict mapping variable names to NCDatasets variables. The global command and filters attributes record the invocation and configured quality-filter rules when provided.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.NetCDFOptions","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.NetCDFOptions","category":"type","text":"NetCDFOptions(; compression=\"deflate\", deflate_level=4, shuffle=false,\n                fill_value=-999f0, chunksizes=nothing, checksum=\"none\",\n                precision=\"float32\", significant_digits_mean=8,\n                significant_digits_std=6, significant_digits_center=5,\n                statistics=[])\n\nNetCDF storage and rounding settings. precision selects \"float32\" or \"float64\" payloads. compression is \"deflate\" or \"none\"; levels are 0–9. fill_value may be a number finite in the selected precision or NaN. Each significant-digit setting accepts a positive integer or \"none\" to disable rounding. Requests above 9 digits for Float32 or 17 for Float64 leave the output value unrounded. To preserve Float64 results exactly, use Float64 accumulators with precision=\"float64\" and set the applicable significant-digit settings to \"none\". See grid_l2 and grid_center for accumulator precision. Chunk sizes follow Julia dimension order, including time (last). By default, chunks contain at most 1024 × 1024 spatial cells (2^20 cells on a flat cell axis), one cube face, and one time. checksum is \"none\" or \"fletcher32\". statistics selects optional L2 NetCDF fields \"sum_w2\" and \"n_soundings\".\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.default_cache_dir","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.default_cache_dir","category":"function","text":"default_cache_dir() -> String\n\nReturn the base cache directory for generated auxiliary data. Override with SATELLITEGRIDDING_CACHE.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.default_modis_cache_dir","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.default_modis_cache_dir","category":"function","text":"default_modis_cache_dir(; pixels=2400) -> String\n\nReturn the default per-tile MODIS sinusoidal geolocation cache directory. Override with SATELLITEGRIDDING_MODIS_GEO_CACHE.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.parse_modis_tile","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.parse_modis_tile","category":"function","text":"parse_modis_tile(path_or_tile) -> (h, v)\n\nParse a MODIS tile identifier such as h08v04 from a filename or string.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.modis_sinusoidal_latlon","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.modis_sinusoidal_latlon","category":"function","text":"modis_sinusoidal_latlon(h, v, line, sample; pixels=2400)\n\nCompute the latitude/longitude at a MODLAND sinusoidal pixel center. h and v are zero-based tile indices; line and sample are one-based pixel indices within the tile.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.generate_modis_tile_geolocation","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.generate_modis_tile_geolocation","category":"function","text":"generate_modis_tile_geolocation(h, v; pixels=2400)\n\nGenerate latitude and longitude arrays for a MODLAND sinusoidal tile from the official projection definition. Arrays are indexed as [line, sample].\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.write_modis_tile_geolocation","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.write_modis_tile_geolocation","category":"function","text":"write_modis_tile_geolocation(path, h, v; pixels=2400, overwrite=false)\n\nGenerate and write one MODIS sinusoidal tile geolocation file. The output is a small per-tile NetCDF file with latitude(y, x) and longitude(y, x).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.load_or_generate_modis_tile_geolocation","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.load_or_generate_modis_tile_geolocation","category":"function","text":"load_or_generate_modis_tile_geolocation(h, v; cache_dir=default_modis_cache_dir(), pixels=2400)\n\nRead a per-tile MODIS geolocation cache file, generating it first if needed. Returns (latitude, longitude) arrays indexed as [line, sample].\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.apply_filters","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.apply_filters","category":"function","text":"apply_filters(dataset, config, lat_bounds, lon_bounds, grid_spec) -> Vector{Int}\n\nReturn the indices of pixels passing all configured quality filters. Rectangular grids also require all four footprint corners strictly inside the domain and a longitude span below 50°. Spherical grids cover the entire sphere, including dateline and polar footprints. Input preparation checks their coordinate validity before accumulation.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.compute_evi","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.compute_evi","category":"function","text":"compute_evi(red, nir, blue; G=2.5f0, C1=6.0f0, C2=7.5f0, L=10000.0f0)\n\nCompute the Enhanced Vegetation Index (EVI).\n\nEVI = G × (NIR - RED) / (NIR + C1×RED - C2×BLUE + L)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.compute_ndvi","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.compute_ndvi","category":"function","text":"compute_ndvi(red, nir)\n\nCompute the Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI).\n\nNDVI = (NIR - RED) / (NIR + RED)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.compute_nirv","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.compute_nirv","category":"function","text":"compute_nirv(red, nir)\n\nCompute the Near-Infrared Reflectance of Vegetation (NIRv).\n\nNIRv = NDVI × NIR\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.compute_ndwi","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.compute_ndwi","category":"function","text":"compute_ndwi(nir, swir)\n\nCompute the Normalized Difference Water Index (NDWI).\n\nNDWI = (NIR - SWIR) / (NIR + SWIR)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.parse_l2_args","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.parse_l2_args","category":"function","text":"parse_l2_args(args=ARGS) -> Dict\n\nParse command-line arguments for L2 gridding. Matches the interface of the original gridL2_Dates.jl script.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.parse_center_args","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.parse_center_args","category":"function","text":"parse_center_args(args=ARGS) -> Dict\n\nParse command-line arguments for center-coordinate gridding (MODIS-style).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.args_to_grid_spec","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.args_to_grid_spec","category":"function","text":"args_to_grid_spec(args::Dict) -> AbstractGridSpec\n\nConvert parsed CLI arguments to a grid spec. Returns a RectangularGridSpec for --gridType rect (default), a CubedSphereGridSpec for --gridType cs (GEOS/GCHP convention by default, requires --Nc), or a HEALPixGridSpec for --gridType healpix with --nside.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"SatelliteGridding.args_to_time_spec","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#SatelliteGridding.args_to_time_spec","category":"function","text":"args_to_time_spec(args::Dict) -> TimeSpec\n\nConvert parsed CLI arguments to a TimeSpec.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"Algorithm","page":"Algorithm","location":"algorithm/#Algorithm","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Footprint Oversampling","page":"Algorithm","location":"algorithm/#Footprint-Oversampling","category":"section","text":"Satellite instruments observe the atmosphere or surface through irregularly shaped footprints. Each footprint is a quadrilateral defined by 4 corner coordinates (latitude/longitude pairs). These quadrilaterals can overlap multiple output grid cells, so simply assigning each observation to its center coordinate would lose spatial information.\n\nSatelliteGridding.jl uses sub-pixel oversampling to properly distribute each observation across the grid cells it covers. The default path treats footprints as quadrilaterals. CircularFootprintGridding treats the four coordinates as a circular-footprint bounding box or edge points.\n\nThe following three steps describe quadrilateral subdivision on rectangular grids."},{"title":"Step 1: Corner Sorting (CCW)","page":"Algorithm","location":"algorithm/#Step-1:-Corner-Sorting-(CCW)","category":"section","text":"Different satellite products store corner coordinates in different orders. TROPOMI uses counter-clockwise (CCW) order, while OCO-2 uses a different convention. Before any geometric computation, corners are sorted into CCW order based on their angle from the footprint centroid:\n\n        3 ──── 2          Sorted CCW:\n       /      /           1 = smallest angle from centroid\n      /      /            2 = next angle\n     4 ──── 1             Edges 1→2 and 4→3 are opposite sides\n\nThe sorting uses a 5-comparator sorting network (optimal for 4 elements), which avoids allocations and works efficiently in GPU kernels."},{"title":"Step 2: Sub-Pixel Computation","page":"Algorithm","location":"algorithm/#Step-2:-Sub-Pixel-Computation","category":"section","text":"The quadrilateral is subdivided into n×n sub-pixels by:\n\nDividing edge 1→2 into n equally-spaced points\nDividing edge 4→3 into n equally-spaced points\nConnecting corresponding points across the two edges\nSubdividing each connecting line into n interior points\n\nThis produces n² sub-pixel positions that fill the footprint. Each sub-pixel carries weight 1/n².\n\n    4 ────────────── 3\n    |  ·  ·  ·  ·  |     n = 4\n    |  ·  ·  ·  ·  |     16 sub-pixels\n    |  ·  ·  ·  ·  |     Each has weight 1/16\n    |  ·  ·  ·  ·  |\n    1 ────────────── 2"},{"title":"Step 3: Accumulation","page":"Algorithm","location":"algorithm/#Step-3:-Accumulation","category":"section","text":"Each sub-pixel is assigned to the grid cell it falls in (via floor of its fractional grid index). The observation value is accumulated into that cell with the sub-pixel's weight on both the sequential and rectangular KA paths.\n\nFast path: If all 4 corners fall in the same grid cell, the footprint is entirely within one cell. The full observation is assigned with weight 1 (no sub-pixel computation needed).\n\nSkip path: If the footprint's longitude extent exceeds n grid cells, it's too wide for meaningful oversampling and is skipped."},{"title":"Spherical Footprints","page":"Algorithm","location":"algorithm/#Spherical-Footprints","category":"section","text":"Cubed-sphere and HEALPix grids convert the corners to unit vectors and order them in a local tangent plane. Every footprint is sampled at the midpoints of a bilinear n × n grid, and each interpolated vector is normalized and located on the sphere. A cell hit by k samples gets one update with weight k/n². Automatic subdivision uses n = clamp(ceil(4 L_max / Δ), 4, 24), where L_max is the longest great-circle span between corners and Δ is the mean cell spacing. See the cubed-sphere and HEALPix pages for geometry and weighting details."},{"title":"Circular Footprints","page":"Algorithm","location":"algorithm/#Circular-Footprints","category":"section","text":"For circular products such as GOSAT, the footprint extent can come from four bounding coordinates or from center latitude/longitude plus a radius. If a corner-based product does not provide explicit center latitude/longitude variables, the center is derived from the four coordinates.\n\nThe circular path samples an n×n grid over the inferred bounding box and keeps only points inside the circle. In fractional grid-index space this can become an ellipse when dLat != dLon, which is the right representation for a circular footprint on a grid whose latitude and longitude cell sizes differ.\n\nFor center-plus-radius products, set [basic] lat and [basic] lon, then either provide a per-sounding [basic] radius variable or a scalar [circle] radius. [circle] radius_unit can be degrees, km, or m.\n\nUse this method from Julia:\n\ngrid(config, grid_spec, time_spec,\n     CircularFootprintGridding(n_oversample=80);\n     outfile=\"gosat_sif.nc\")\n\nor from the CLI:\n\njulia --project=. bin/grid.jl l2 \\\n    --config examples/gosat_sif_center_radius.toml \\\n    --footprint circle --nOversample 80 \\\n    --backend cpu \\\n    -o gosat_sif.nc\n\nThis method works with the sequential backend and the KA CPU/CUDA/Metal backends. A footprint contributes k / inside_count to a cell with k hits. The denominator counts all samples inside the inferred circle or ellipse. Out-of-grid samples are dropped without renormalizing the remaining weights."},{"title":"Choosing n on Rectangular Grids","page":"Algorithm","location":"algorithm/#Choosing-n-on-Rectangular-Grids","category":"section","text":"With automatic oversampling, each batch uses the median of its footprints' larger latitude or longitude extent measured in grid cells.\n\nn = clamp(ceil(3 × median_extent), 2, 20)\n\nThis aims for ~3 sub-pixels per grid cell width. Typical values:\n\nTROPOMI at 1° grid: n ≈ 3\nTROPOMI at 0.05° grid: n ≈ 10–15\nOCO-2 at 1° grid: n ≈ 2"},{"title":"Averaging Methods","page":"Algorithm","location":"algorithm/#Averaging-Methods","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Sequential Path (Welford)","page":"Algorithm","location":"algorithm/#Sequential-Path-(Welford)","category":"section","text":"The default sequential path uses Welford's online algorithm for computing the running mean and variance in a single pass:\n\nfor each observation x with weight w:\n    total_weight += w\n    delta = x - mean\n    mean += (w / total_weight) * delta\n    M2 += w * delta * (x - mean)    # only if compute_std=true\n\nThis is numerically stable even for values with large magnitudes, as it never computes large sums. The standard deviation is: std = sqrt(M2 / total_weight)."},{"title":"KA Path (Shifted Sums)","page":"Algorithm","location":"algorithm/#KA-Path-(Shifted-Sums)","category":"section","text":"The KernelAbstractions path accumulates weights and shifted values in parallel. For each variable, the reference is the median of the values finite in accumulator precision in the first nonempty batch. A variable with no finite values in that batch uses zero. Tiled runs choose a reference per tile. Rolling runs share one reference across days.\n\nfor each observation x with weight w:\n    shifted_sum += w * (x - reference)\n    W += w\n\npreliminary_mean = reference + shifted_sum / W\n\nWith Float32 accumulators or when standard deviation is requested, the first pass also records the observed cell minimum and maximum. The driver clamps the finite preliminary cell mean to those bounds, then replays the input files. For each cell with preliminary mean m, replay accumulates the linear residual r and, when computing standard deviation, the squared residual q.\n\nr = sum(w * (x - m))\nq = sum(w * (x - m)^2)\nmean = m + r / W\nM2 = max(q - r^2 / W, 0)\nstd = sqrt(M2 / W)\n\nWith Float32 accumulators, the driver also clamps the refined mean to the observed bounds.\n\nMean-only Float64 runs use the first pass alone. Rolling windows merge centered daily moments in Float64 with Chan's formula. Mean-only Float64 KA windows add the daily shifted sums in Float64 before restoring the reference."},{"title":"Pipeline Architecture","page":"Algorithm","location":"algorithm/#Pipeline-Architecture","category":"section","text":"Input files (NetCDF)\n    │\n    ▼\n┌─────────────────────────┐\n│  File Discovery         │  Date-based pattern matching (YYYY/MM/DD)\n│  (filepatterns.jl)      │\n└──────────┬──────────────┘\n           │\n           ▼\n┌─────────────────────────┐\n│  Quality Filtering      │  Apply filter rules from [filter] section\n│  (filters.jl)           │\n└──────────┬──────────────┘\n           │\n           ▼\n┌─────────────────────────┐\n│  Corner Sorting (CCW)   │  Normalize corner order\n│  (oversampling.jl)      │\n└──────────┬──────────────┘\n           │\n           ▼\n┌─────────────────────────┐\n│  Sub-Pixel Indices      │  Compute n×n grid cell indices per footprint\n│  (oversampling.jl)      │\n└──────────┬──────────────┘\n           │\n           ▼\n┌─────────────────────────┐\n│  Accumulation           │  Welford (sequential) or sum-based (KA)\n│  (averaging.jl)         │\n└──────────┬──────────────┘\n           │\n           ▼\n┌─────────────────────────┐\n│  Output (NetCDF)        │  Write gridded means, counts, optional std\n│  (ncio.jl)              │\n└──────────────────────────┘"},{"title":"Cubed-Sphere Grids (GEOS / GCHP)","page":"Cubed-Sphere Grids","location":"cubed_sphere/#Cubed-Sphere-Grids-(GEOS-/-GCHP)","category":"section","text":"In addition to regular latitude/longitude grids (RectangularGridSpec), SatelliteGridding can grid Level-2 footprints directly onto a cubed-sphere output grid in the GEOS / GCHP convention via CubedSphereGridSpec. This is useful for comparing satellite observations against GEOS-Chem (GCHP) output on its native grid without an intermediate regridding step."},{"title":"Convention","page":"Cubed-Sphere Grids","location":"cubed_sphere/#Convention","category":"section","text":"The default geometry is GMAOCubedSphereDefinition — the grid used by GEOS-IT / GEOS-FP and by GCHP when driven by GMAO meteorology:\n\nequal-distance gnomonic edge coordinate law (grid_type = 0 in GEOS/FV),\nfour-corner-normalized cell centers (GEOS cell_center2),\nGEOS-native panel ordering (panels 1–2 equatorial, 3 north pole, 4–5 equatorial, 6 south pole), and\na final −10° longitude shift.\n\nAn EquiangularCubedSphereDefinition (classic tan-spaced equiangular gnomonic, no offset) is also available for synthetic targets.\n\nA grid is named C<Nc>, where Nc is the number of cells per panel edge. The cube has 6·Nc² cells total. For example, Nc = 360 is C360 (~25 km). Input geodetic latitudes are used directly as spherical latitudes, as on the HEALPix grid.\n\nnote: Projection provenance\nThe closed-form gnomonic projection and its inverse (lonlat_to_panel_xy) are adapted from the AtmosTransportModel package (src/Grids/CubedSphereMesh.jl, commit 6522d85). See src/cubed_sphere.jl for the full attribution."},{"title":"Library usage","page":"Cubed-Sphere Grids","location":"cubed_sphere/#Library-usage","category":"section","text":"using SatelliteGridding, Dates\n\nconfig    = load_config(\"examples/tropomi_sif.toml\")\ngrid_spec = CubedSphereGridSpec(; Nc = 360)             # C360, GMAO/GCHP default\ntime_spec = TimeSpec(DateTime(\"2020-07-01\"), DateTime(\"2020-07-16\"), Day(16))\n\ngrid(config, grid_spec, time_spec, SubpixelGridding();\n     outfile = \"tropomi_sif_c360.nc\", keep_going = true)\n\nThe data-source config (tropomi_sif.toml) is grid-agnostic — only the grid_spec changes between a regular and a cubed-sphere run. See examples/tropomi_sif_cubedsphere.jl for a runnable C24 demo."},{"title":"CLI usage","page":"Cubed-Sphere Grids","location":"cubed_sphere/#CLI-usage","category":"section","text":"julia --project=. bin/grid.jl l2 \\\n    --config examples/tropomi_sif.toml \\\n    --gridType cs --Nc 360 \\\n    --startDate 2020-07-01 --stopDate 2020-07-16 --dDays 16 \\\n    -o tropomi_sif_c360.nc\n\n--csConvention selects gmao (default) or equiangular. For long global runs, bin/run_tropomi_c360.sh fans the work out across time chunks (one Julia process per chunk) and concatenates with ncrcat:\n\nNC=360 JOBS=8 bash bin/run_tropomi_c360.sh"},{"title":"How footprints are gridded","page":"Cubed-Sphere Grids","location":"cubed_sphere/#How-footprints-are-gridded","category":"section","text":"The cube and HEALPix share the same spherical footprint subdivision. The four corners become unit vectors and are ordered in the footprint's tangent plane. The sampler evaluates the centres of an n × n bilinear parameter grid and normalizes each interpolated vector. The cube locator projects each sample independently, so the same subdivision works across the antimeridian and panel edges and for footprints that enclose a pole. A cell hit by k samples receives one update with weight k/n², its sampled fraction of the footprint's bilinear parameter domain. The weights for each valid footprint sum to one. Equal subpoint weights correspond to uniform angular sampling within the detector's instantaneous field of view (IFOV). For realistic OCO and TROPOMI pushbroom taper of up to about 0.5%, the estimated difference from spherical-area cell weights is about 0.1% or less. See the HEALPix weighting discussion for details.\n\nAutomatic subdivision chooses n = clamp(ceil(4 L_max / Δ), 4, 24) for each footprint. Here L_max is the largest great-circle distance between any pair of corners and Δ = sqrt(4πR²/(6 Nc²)) is the mean cell spacing on a sphere of radius R. At high resolution the largest cells have about 4/√3 ≈ 2.31 times the area of the smallest on GMAO and √2 ≈ 1.41 times on equiangular grids. The narrowest GMAO corner cells are about 0.74 Δ wide. Below the cap, the nominal sample spacing is at most Δ/4 along the longest span, giving about three samples per narrow corner-cell width. The cap limits n to 24 once L_max exceeds 6 Δ, about 154 km at C360 or 77 km at C720 on a sphere of radius 6,371 km. Use --nOversample or SubpixelGridding(n_oversample=...) to set n explicitly for wide footprints."},{"title":"Backends and precision","page":"Cubed-Sphere Grids","location":"cubed_sphere/#Backends-and-precision","category":"section","text":"Quadrilateral gridding supports the sequential backend and KernelAbstractions on KA CPU, CUDA, and Metal, including tiled and rolling windows and standard deviation. Select an accelerator with backend=resolve_backend(\"cuda\") or --backend cuda.\n\nSequential geometry uses Float64. KA CPU and CUDA default to Float64 for both geometry and accumulation. Set geometry_precision and accumulator_precision independently in grid_l2 (CLI --geometry-precision and --accumulator-precision). Metal requires Float32 for both. As on HEALPix, Float32 geometry rounds the corner vectors, interpolation and projection. In a C720 test with 500,000 uniformly distributed points per definition, the Float32 locator changed 0.0030% of GMAO cell assignments and 0.0034% of equiangular assignments relative to Float64. A C720 scan of every panel's horizontal and vertical grid lines at each cell-edge midpoint found assignment changes up to about 1.42 m from a GMAO edge and 0.93 m from an equiangular edge, including Float32 rounding of the input unit vectors. The GMAO inverse adds opposite-signed terms near one panel edge to compute its fractional index, which contributes to the shift. Exact edge and corner ties can choose a different incident cell. Footprint interpolation and adaptive sample-count rounding can also affect weights close to cell boundaries. At very large Nc (around 2^23 and above), Float32 fractional cell indices can round by half a cell or more. Use Float64 geometry at those resolutions.\n\nThe grid's T controls the precision of the precomputed cell centre and corner coordinates. CubedSphereGridSpec with T=Float32 computes these in Float32 and widens them to Float64 for NetCDF. Set T=Float64 for double-precision coordinates and for Float64 accumulation in tiled sequential runs."},{"title":"Output layout","page":"Cubed-Sphere Grids","location":"cubed_sphere/#Output-layout","category":"section","text":"The NetCDF output matches native GEOS / GCHP files, so it opens directly in Panoply, xarray, and GCHP tooling:\n\nItem Details\nDimensions Xdim = Ydim = Nc, nf = 6, XCdim = YCdim = Nc+1, unlimited time\nData variables (time, nf, Ydim, Xdim), with coordinates = \"lons lats\" and grid_mapping = \"cubed_sphere\"\nCell centers 2D lons(nf, Ydim, Xdim), lats(...) in degrees\nCell corners corner_lons(nf, YCdim, XCdim), corner_lats(...)\nFake 1D axes Xdim/Ydim (degrees, GrADS compatibility); nf = 1..6\nWeights n(time, nf, Ydim, Xdim) stores the sum of fractional footprint weights\n\nIn xarray you can plot a face directly on its 2D coordinates:\n\nimport xarray as xr\nds = xr.open_dataset(\"tropomi_sif_c360.nc\")\nds[\"sif_743\"].isel(time=0, nf=2).plot(x=\"lons\", y=\"lats\")   # one panel"},{"title":"HEALPix Grids","page":"HEALPix Grids","location":"healpix/#HEALPix-Grids","category":"section","text":"HEALPixGridSpec provides global gridding in HEALPix NESTED order with equal solid angle per cell on the sphere. The resolution is nside = 2^level, with 12 * nside^2 cells and zero-based Int64 cell IDs. The four children of cell p at the next level have IDs 4p:4p+3. The parent ID of a child c is c >> 2. HEALPix geometry and numbering, Górski et al. (2005).\n\nInput geodetic latitudes are used directly as spherical latitudes, as for the cubed-sphere grid, so the grids use the same latitude convention. True WGS84 cell areas vary by about 1.35% from equator to pole, from −0.45% at the equator to +0.90% at the poles relative to the spherical cell_area. Longitude is periodic. Input longitude is canonicalized in degrees before locating cells, and cell centre and vertex longitudes are in [0, 360)."},{"title":"Usage","page":"HEALPix Grids","location":"healpix/#Usage","category":"section","text":"using SatelliteGridding, Dates\n\nconfig = load_config(\"examples/tropomi_sif.toml\")\nspec = HEALPixGridSpec(nside=64, cell_bounds=true)\ntimes = TimeSpec(DateTime(\"2020-07-01\"), DateTime(\"2020-07-16\"), Day(16))\ngrid_l2(config, spec, times; n_oversample=8, compute_std=true,\n        outfile=\"tropomi_healpix.nc\")\n\njulia --project=. bin/grid.jl l2 \\\n    --config examples/tropomi_sif.toml \\\n    --gridType healpix --nside 64 --nOversample 8 \\\n    --startDate 2020-07-01 --stopDate 2020-07-16 --dDays 16 \\\n    --outFile tropomi_healpix.nc\n\nEvery footprint is sampled, including those whose four corners fall in a single cell. Corners are converted to unit vectors and ordered counterclockwise in the tangent plane at their normalized vector sum. The four vectors are interpolated bilinearly at ((i - 0.5)/n, (j - 0.5)/n) and the resulting vectors are normalized to locate the subpoints on the sphere. This handles footprints crossing the dateline and those enclosing a pole.\n\nSubpoints carry equal weight 1/n² in the bilinear unit-vector parameterization, corresponding to uniform angular sampling within the detector's instantaneous field of view (IFOV). For parallelogram footprints this gives spherical-area weighting to footprint-scale accuracy, with a small correction from radial normalization. For tapered footprints the difference follows the taper. For realistic OCO and TROPOMI pushbroom taper of up to about 0.5%, the estimated difference in cell weights is about 0.1% or less, roughly 30 times smaller than finite-sampling error in the swath-edge tests.\n\nThe gridding loop counts the k subpoints in each cell and updates its statistics once per footprint with weight k/n². Footprints entirely inside one cell contribute exactly one unit of weight. The resulting per-footprint cell overlaps also supply the optional sum_w2 and n_soundings statistics.\n\nBy default n = clamp(ceil(Int, 4 * L_max / d_cell), 4, 24) per footprint. L_max is the largest great-circle distance between any two footprint corners and d_cell = sqrt(4πR² / npix) is the mean cell spacing on a sphere of radius R. An explicit n_oversample in Julia or --nOversample on the CLI uses a fixed integer from 1 to 1024 samples per side. The upper clamp can leave interior cells unsampled. Use an explicit n_oversample for coarse sounders with L_max ≳ 75 km at nside = 2048, or for TROPOMI swath-edge footprints at nside ≥ 8192."},{"title":"GPU and KernelAbstractions","page":"HEALPix Grids","location":"healpix/#GPU-and-KernelAbstractions","category":"section","text":"Use backend=resolve_backend(\"cuda\"), resolve_backend(\"metal\"), or resolve_backend(\"cpu\") in Julia, or --backend cuda, --backend metal, or --backend cpu on the CLI. All three support quadrilateral footprints, tiled and rolling time windows, and compute_std=true (--compSTD). Standard deviation rereads the files in each tile or rolling-cache day.\n\nCUDA and KA CPU use Float64 geometry by default to match sequential cell assignment. Set geometry_precision=Float32 (--geometry-precision float32) to use single precision. Set accumulator_precision (--accumulator-precision float32 or float64) independently for weights, shifted sums, deviations, and cached daily moments. Accumulators default to Float64 on CUDA and KA CPU. Metal uses Float32 for both settings and rejects Float64. Float32 geometry quantizes the input corner vectors and orders them in the tangent plane in the same precision. It can move samples across cell edges or change the adaptive sample count near its rounding thresholds. At nside=2048, the Float32 cell-edge shift is at most about 0.33 m. For representative OCO and TROPOMI footprints, the total subpoint displacement is about 0.2 m RMS and at most about 1.1 m, below the typical 50–500 m L2 geolocation uncertainty. In sparsely grazed perimeter cells (n ≪ 1), one flipped subpoint can change occupancy or shift the mean by an amount comparable to the scene standard deviation. In the XCO₂ examples, cell means with n ≳ 2 shifted by about 0.05 ppm or less. The first nonempty batch supplies a per-variable median reference for the shifted first pass. Float32 KA runs replay the files to refine each cell's mean using deviations around it. Float64 KA runs replay when standard deviation is requested. GPU Acceleration describes precision limits, memory use, and the spherical grid locator interface."},{"title":"NetCDF layout","page":"HEALPix Grids","location":"healpix/#NetCDF-layout","category":"section","text":"Gridded variables, weight sums n, and optional *_std all use (cell, time) in Julia and (time, cell) in xarray or CDL. They reference the healpix grid mapping and the lat lon cell_ids coordinates. Files declare Conventions = \"CF-1.13\". The cell_ids variable holds the NESTED IDs with standard_name = \"healpix_index\", units = \"1\", and xdggs metadata (grid_name = \"healpix\", level, indexing_scheme = \"nested\", and ellipsoid = \"sphere\"). The scalar cell_area gives the common area in square metres on a sphere of radius 6,371 km. CF 1.13, Appendix F.\n\nn is dimensionless and sums fractional footprint overlap weights, so a sounding split across cells contributes a fraction to each cell. The optional *_std is the weighted population standard deviation, sqrt(M2 / n), where M2 is the weighted sum of squared deviations from the cell mean. It has the source variable's units. A cell touched by a single footprint has zero standard deviation when the moments are finite. Spherical KA writes fill when the variance or its inputs become non-finite in accumulator precision. The calculation uses no Bessel correction. With Float32 accumulators, including on Metal, roundoff can erase small standard deviations, especially in cells with many contributions. See the GPU precision discussion.\n\nNetCDF stores n = 0 for unobserved cells, including entirely empty time slices, and reserves -1 for unwritten weights. A cell with n > 0 can still contain science fill, for example when its standard deviation overflows.\n\nL2 NetCDF records accumulator_precision and geometry_precision as global attributes. For gridding inputs to coarsening or regridding, use the exact-archive recipe.\n\nWith the optional sum_w2 and n_soundings fields, fractional overlaps act as reliability weights for independent soundings with a common mean and variance. Require sum_w2 > 0, n_soundings ≥ 2, and N_eff > 1, where Kish N_eff = n² / sum_w2. The unbiased sample variance is s² = M2 / (n − sum_w2/n) = std² * N_eff / (N_eff − 1) and the standard error is SE = std / √(N_eff − 1). Avoid M2 / (n − 1) and std / √n. Overlapping rolling slices reuse soundings, and soundings shared across cells induce covariance. Overlap statistics configuration.\n\nAll rolling windows with standard deviation merge centered daily moments in Float64, as do mean-only Float32 KA runs. Float64 KA restores the shared reference after merging. Float32 KA replay supplies the bounded absolute mean to the merge.\n\nFor xdggs, open the file with xarray and use xdggs.decode(ds.set_coords(\"cell_ids\")) to decode the ID coordinate. xdggs HEALPix tutorial.\n\nFloat64 lon and lat hold cell centres. Set cell_bounds=true in Julia to also write Float64 lon_bounds and lat_bounds with Julia dimensions (nv, cell). The four corner vertices follow healpy's north, west, south, east order and are intended for plotting. Conservative regridding needs the curved HEALPix edges. Connecting the vertices by great circles changes the area of cells touching a pole by about 10%. Coordinate arrays are generated in chunks during file creation. Gridding uses dense accumulators, so memory still grows with the cell count and the number of variables."},{"title":"Coarsening a NESTED archive","page":"HEALPix Grids","location":"healpix/#Coarsening-a-NESTED-archive","category":"section","text":"Use coarsen_healpix(\"fine.nc\", \"coarse.nc\"; levels=2) in Julia, or pass nside=… to select a parent resolution directly. Each level halves nside and combines four consecutive NESTED cells into one parent. The output includes every time slice, the original time and window coordinates, and recomputed HEALPix coordinates and bounds (if present). The global attributes coarsen_source, coarsen_source_nside, coarsen_target_nside, and coarsen_levels record the original archive, its resolution, the output resolution, and the total levels removed across all coarsening steps.\n\nThe coarsener uses satellitegridding_role to identify weights and science fields and satellitegridding_mean and satellitegridding_std to link each mean and its deviation. These tags take precedence over legacy_std. Untagged archives without <name> and <name>_std pairs coarsen as means. When a pair exists, the default legacy_std=:error stops before creating output because <name>_std could be an independent science field.\n\nUse legacy_std=:by_name for archives written with compute_std=true. Every science mean must have a matching population standard deviation on (cell, time), and the pairs must be disjoint.\nUse legacy_std=:none to coarsen every science field as an independent mean, including fields whose names end in _std.\n\nFor each parent, the coarsener sums child weights and combines child means and central moments with Chan's formula in Float64. It recovers each child's central moment as M2 = n * std^2 and writes the parent's weighted population standard deviation as sqrt(M2 / n). It skips cells with n = 0. A missing mean in a positive-weight child propagates to the parent mean and standard deviation for that variable. A missing child standard deviation propagates to the parent standard deviation."},{"title":"Exact-archive recipe","page":"HEALPix Grids","location":"healpix/#Exact-archive-recipe","category":"section","text":"Grid the fine archive with Float64 accumulation and spherical geometry. In Julia, use HEALPixGridSpec(; nside=..., T=Float64) for sequential gridding. On the sequential CLI, use --accumulator-precision float64. KA CPU and CUDA default to Float64 for both precisions. Enable standard deviations with compute_std=true in Julia or --compSTD on the CLI.\n\n[output]\nprecision = \"float64\"\nsignificant_digits_mean = \"none\"\nsignificant_digits_std = \"none\"\nfill_value = nan\n\nCoarsening preserves the moments of the archived sub-sample set up to Float64 summation and serialization roundoff, including with adaptive oversampling. To reproduce those moments by gridding directly at the parent resolution, use the same fixed n_oversample (--nOversample on the CLI), geometry precision, and time windows for the two runs. Adaptive oversampling can choose different sub-samples at each resolution. Quantization of nearly equal serialized child means can dominate a small parent standard deviation. Coarsening writes Float64 without decimal rounding and warns about Float32 accumulation, Float32 storage, decimal rounding, and missing precision or rounding metadata."},{"title":"Coarsened overlap statistics","page":"HEALPix Grids","location":"healpix/#Coarsened-overlap-statistics","category":"section","text":"When present, sum_w2 and n_soundings are summed across descendants and marked with coarsening_approximation. A missing statistic in any observed child propagates to that parent statistic. For L total levels removed, compare with parent statistics for the same archived sub-samples. The summed sum_w2 is between 4⁻ᴸ and 1 times the true parent value, while the summed n_soundings is between 1 and 4ᴸ times the true parent count. A footprint touching multiple descendants contributes multiple counts and loses the cross-products of its weights. This can inflate N_eff by up to 4ᴸ. Do not use these sums for sample variance or SE. Even one footprint can pass both estimator guards after coarsening and give a spurious zero SE. Grid directly at the parent resolution for these estimates.\n\nIn a one-day OCO-2 run on CUDA with Float64 and no decimal rounding, using n_oversample=4, coarsening from nside=512 to 128 matched direct nside=128 gridding with zero difference in n, a maximum mean difference of 6 × 10⁻¹⁴ ppm, and a maximum relative standard deviation difference of 9 × 10⁻¹⁴. The summed n_soundings was 20% higher (97,157 vs 81,077) and the summed sum_w2 was 6.6% lower (69,099 vs 73,950)."},{"title":"Known limitations and planned work","page":"HEALPix Grids","location":"healpix/#Known-limitations-and-planned-work","category":"section","text":"HEALPix supports quadrilateral Level-2 footprints to NetCDF. Circular footprints, centre-coordinate gridding such as MODIS, Zarr output, and UGRID export are outside this support.\n\nPer-cell WGS84 ellipsoidal areas are planned separately."},{"title":"Tutorial: Gridding TROPOMI SIF Data","page":"Tutorial","location":"generated/tutorial/#Tutorial:-Gridding-TROPOMI-SIF-Data","category":"section","text":"This tutorial walks through gridding TROPOMI Solar-Induced Fluorescence (SIF) data onto a regular lat/lon grid using SatelliteGridding.jl."},{"title":"Setup","page":"Tutorial","location":"generated/tutorial/#Setup","category":"section","text":"First, load the package and define the time range:\n\nusing SatelliteGridding\nusing Dates"},{"title":"Step 1: Load the Configuration","page":"Tutorial","location":"generated/tutorial/#Step-1:-Load-the-Configuration","category":"section","text":"Configuration files (TOML format) tell the package where to find data files, which NetCDF variables to read, and what quality filters to apply.\n\nHere's what a typical TROPOMI SIF config looks like:\n\nfilePattern = \"S5P_PAL__L2B_SIF____YYYYMMDD*.nc\"\nfolder = \"/path/to/tropomi/sif/\"\n\n[basic]\nlat = \"PRODUCT/latitude\"\nlon = \"PRODUCT/longitude\"\nlat_bnd = \"PRODUCT/SUPPORT_DATA/GEOLOCATIONS/latitude_bounds\"\nlon_bnd = \"PRODUCT/SUPPORT_DATA/GEOLOCATIONS/longitude_bounds\"\n\n[grid]\nsif_743 = \"PRODUCT/SIF_743\"\nsif_735 = \"PRODUCT/SIF_735\"\n\n[filter]\n\"PRODUCT/SUPPORT_DATA/GEOLOCATIONS/solar_zenith_angle\" = \"< 80\"\n\nconfig = load_config(joinpath(pkgdir(SatelliteGridding), \"examples\", \"tropomi_sif.toml\"))\nDataSourceConfig(Dict(\"lat\" => \"PRODUCT/latitude\", \"lat_bnd\" => \"PRODUCT/SUPPORT_DATA/GEOLOCATIONS/latitude_bounds\", \"lon\" => \"PRODUCT/longitude\", \"lon_bnd\" => \"PRODUCT/SUPPORT_DATA/GEOLOCATIONS/longitude_bounds\"), OrderedCollections.OrderedDict(\"sif_735_corr\" => \"PRODUCT/SIF_Corr_735\", \"sif_743\" => \"PRODUCT/SIF_743\", \"sif_743_err\" => \"PRODUCT/SIF_ERROR_743\", \"sif_735\" => \"PRODUCT/SIF_735\", \"sif_743_corr\" => \"PRODUCT/SIF_Corr_743\", \"cloud_fraction\" => \"PRODUCT/SUPPORT_DATA/INPUT_DATA/cloud_fraction_L2\"), FilterRule[FilterRule(\"PRODUCT/SUPPORT_DATA/GEOLOCATIONS/solar_zenith_angle\", :lt, 80.0, NaN)], \"S5P_PAL__L2B_SIF____YYYYMMDD*.nc\", \"/kiwi-data/Data/satellite/TROPOMI/TROPOMI_SIF_S5P-PAL/original/\", Dict{String, Any}())\n\nThe config object contains:\n\nbasic: Coordinate variable paths (lat, lon, and corner bounds)\ngrid_vars: Variables to be gridded (e.g., SIF at 743nm and 735nm)\nfilters: Quality filter rules (here: solar zenith angle < 80°)\nfile_pattern: Glob pattern with date placeholders\nfolder: Root data directory"},{"title":"Step 2: Define the Output Grid","page":"Tutorial","location":"generated/tutorial/#Step-2:-Define-the-Output-Grid","category":"section","text":"A GridSpec defines the spatial extent and resolution of the output grid. All values are in degrees. The package uses Float32 for memory efficiency.\n\ngrid_spec = GridSpec(\n    lat_min = -60f0,    # Southern bound\n    lat_max =  80f0,    # Northern bound\n    lon_min = -180f0,   # Western bound\n    lon_max =  180f0,   # Eastern bound\n    dlat = 0.5f0,       # Latitude resolution (degrees)\n    dlon = 0.5f0        # Longitude resolution (degrees)\n)\nRectangularGridSpec{Float32}(-60.0f0, 80.0f0, -180.0f0, 180.0f0, 0.5f0, 0.5f0, Float32[-59.75, -59.25, -58.75, -58.25, -57.75, -57.25, -56.75, -56.25, -55.75, -55.25  …  75.25, 75.75, 76.25, 76.75, 77.25, 77.75, 78.25, 78.75, 79.25, 79.75], Float32[-179.75, -179.25, -178.75, -178.25, -177.75, -177.25, -176.75, -176.25, -175.75, -175.25  …  175.25, 175.75, 176.25, 176.75, 177.25, 177.75, 178.25, 178.75, 179.25, 179.75])\n\nThis creates a 720×280 grid. The grid_spec.lat and grid_spec.lon vectors contain cell center coordinates.\n\nprintln(\"Grid dimensions: $(length(grid_spec.lon)) × $(length(grid_spec.lat))\")\nGrid dimensions: 720 × 280"},{"title":"Step 3: Define the Time Specification","page":"Tutorial","location":"generated/tutorial/#Step-3:-Define-the-Time-Specification","category":"section","text":"A TimeSpec controls temporal binning. You can use Dates.Day or Dates.Month for the time step.\n\ntime_spec = TimeSpec(\n    DateTime(\"2019-07-01\"),     # Start date\n    DateTime(\"2019-07-31\"),     # Stop date\n    Dates.Day(16)               # 16-day composites\n)\nTimeSpec(Dates.DateTime(\"2019-07-01T00:00:00\"), Dates.DateTime(\"2019-07-31T00:00:00\"), Dates.Day(16), 1.0f0, nothing, nothing)\n\nFor monthly composites:\n\ntime_spec = TimeSpec(DateTime(\"2019-01-01\"), DateTime(\"2019-12-31\"),\n                     Dates.Month(1))"},{"title":"Step 4: Run the Gridding","page":"Tutorial","location":"generated/tutorial/#Step-4:-Run-the-Gridding","category":"section","text":"The grid_l2 function processes all matching files, applies filters, subdivides footprints, and writes the output NetCDF file.\n\n# grid_l2(config, grid_spec, time_spec;\n#         outfile = \"tropomi_sif_july2019.nc\",\n#         n_oversample = 10)    # 10×10 sub-pixels per footprint"},{"title":"Key Options","page":"Tutorial","location":"generated/tutorial/#Key-Options","category":"section","text":"n_oversample: Sub-pixel subdivision factor. Higher = more accurate spatial distribution but slower. Default: auto-computed from footprint/grid ratio.\ncompute_std: Set to true to also compute per-cell standard deviation.\nbackend: Compute backend — nothing (sequential Welford), CPU() (KA parallel), CUDABackend() (NVIDIA GPU), MetalBackend() (Apple GPU), or another compatible KernelAbstractions backend."},{"title":"Using the KA Backend","page":"Tutorial","location":"generated/tutorial/#Using-the-KA-Backend","category":"section","text":"For large datasets, the KernelAbstractions backend parallelizes the computation:\n\nusing KernelAbstractions\ngrid_l2(config, grid_spec, time_spec;\n        outfile = \"output.nc\",\n        backend = CPU())\n\nThe KA backend uses sum-based accumulation (instead of Welford's incremental mean), which is fully parallelizable. The mean is computed at the end: mean = sum / weight."},{"title":"Circular Footprints (GOSAT)","page":"Tutorial","location":"generated/tutorial/#Circular-Footprints-(GOSAT)","category":"section","text":"Some products, such as GOSAT SIF, describe a circular footprint with four bounding coordinates or with center coordinates plus a radius. Use CircularFootprintGridding for those products:\n\ngosat_config = load_config(\"examples/gosat_sif_center_radius.toml\")\ngrid(gosat_config, grid_spec, time_spec,\n     CircularFootprintGridding(n_oversample=80);\n     backend=resolve_backend(\"cpu\"),\n     outfile=\"gosat_sif.nc\")\n\nFor center-plus-radius configs, set [basic] lat, [basic] lon, and either [basic] radius or scalar [circle] radius."},{"title":"Step 5: Inspect the Output","page":"Tutorial","location":"generated/tutorial/#Step-5:-Inspect-the-Output","category":"section","text":"The output NetCDF file contains:\n\nlat, lon: Grid cell center coordinates\ntime: Time step dates\nn: Number of observations per cell\nOne variable per entry in [grid] (e.g., sif_743, sif_735)\nOptional _std suffix variables if compute_std=true\n\nusing NCDatasets\nds = Dataset(\"tropomi_sif_july2019.nc\")\nsif = ds[\"sif_743\"][:, :, 1]   # First time step\nweights = ds[\"n\"][:, :, 1]\nclose(ds)"},{"title":"Center-Coordinate Gridding (MODIS)","page":"Tutorial","location":"generated/tutorial/#Center-Coordinate-Gridding-(MODIS)","category":"section","text":"For instruments without footprint bounds (e.g., MODIS), use grid_center:\n\nconfig = load_config(\"examples/modis_reflectance.toml\")\ngrid_spec = GridSpec(dlat=0.05f0, dlon=0.05f0)\ntime_spec = TimeSpec(DateTime(\"2019-01-01\"), DateTime(\"2019-12-31\"),\n                     Dates.Day(1))\ngrid_center(config, grid_spec, time_spec;\n            geo_provider=:modis,\n            veg_indices=true,\n            outfile=\"modis_2019.nc\")"},{"title":"Cubed-Sphere (GEOS/GCHP) Output Grid","page":"Tutorial","location":"generated/tutorial/#Cubed-Sphere-(GEOS/GCHP)-Output-Grid","category":"section","text":"To compare TROPOMI directly against GEOS-Chem (GCHP) output on its native grid, swap the rectangular GridSpec for a CubedSphereGridSpec — everything else (the config, the grid call) is identical. The default geometry is the GEOS/GCHP convention (equal-distance gnomonic, GEOS-native panels, -10° shift), so Nc = 360 gives a GCHP-style C360 grid. Here we build a small C24 grid:\n\ncs_spec = CubedSphereGridSpec(; Nc = 24)   # GMAOCubedSphereDefinition() by default\nCubedSphereGridSpec{Float32, CubedSphereDefinition{GMAOEqualDistanceGnomonic, FourCornerNormalizedCenter, GEOSNativePanelConvention}}(24, CubedSphereDefinition{GMAOEqualDistanceGnomonic, FourCornerNormalizedCenter, GEOSNativePanelConvention}(GMAOEqualDistanceGnomonic(), FourCornerNormalizedCenter(), GEOSNativePanelConvention(), -10.0, :gmao_equal_distance), Float32[306.5737 306.57568 … 306.57568 306.5737; 309.80566 309.80762 … 309.80762 309.80566; … ; 30.194338 30.192373 … 30.192373 30.194338; 33.426285 33.424316 … 33.424316 33.426285;;; 36.573715 36.575684 … 36.575684 36.573715; 39.80567 39.807632 … 39.807632 39.80567; … ; 120.194336 120.19237 … 120.19237 120.194336; 123.426285 123.42432 … 123.42432 123.426285;;; 35.0 31.755074 … 308.24493 305.0; 38.244923 35.0 … 305.0 301.75507; … ; 121.75507 125.0 … 215.0 218.24493; 125.0 128.24493 … 211.75507 215.0;;; 126.57372 129.80566 … 210.19434 213.42628; 126.57569 129.80763 … 210.19237 213.42432; … ; 126.57569 129.80763 … 210.19237 213.42432; 126.57372 129.80566 … 210.19434 213.42628;;; 216.57372 219.80566 … 300.19434 303.42627; 216.57568 219.80763 … 300.19238 303.42432; … ; 216.57568 219.80763 … 300.19238 303.42432; 216.57372 219.80566 … 300.19434 303.42627;;; 215.0 218.24493 … 301.75507 305.0; 211.75507 215.0 … 305.0 308.24493; … ; 128.24493 125.0 … 35.0 31.755074; 125.0 121.75507 … 38.244926 35.0], Float32[-34.505444 -31.534676 … 31.534676 34.505436; -35.865456 -32.83642 … 32.83642 35.865456; … ; -35.865456 -32.83642 … 32.83642 35.865456; -34.505447 -31.534676 … 31.534676 34.505444;;; -34.505444 -31.534676 … 31.534676 34.505436; -35.865456 -32.83642 … 32.83642 35.865456; … ; -35.865456 -32.83642 … 32.83642 35.865456; -34.505447 -31.534676 … 31.534676 34.505444;;; 36.761856 38.24521 … 38.24521 36.761856; 38.24521 39.92845 … 39.92845 38.24521; … ; 38.24521 39.92845 … 39.92845 38.24521; 36.761856 38.24521 … 38.24521 36.761856;;; 34.505444 35.865456 … 35.865456 34.505447; 31.53468 32.83642 … 32.83642 31.534676; … ; -31.53468 -32.83642 … -32.83642 -31.534676; -34.505436 -35.865456 … -35.865456 -34.505444;;; 34.505444 35.865456 … 35.865456 34.505447; 31.53468 32.83642 … 32.83642 31.534676; … ; -31.53468 -32.83642 … -32.83642 -31.534676; -34.505436 -35.865456 … -35.865456 -34.505444;;; -36.761856 -38.24521 … -38.24521 -36.761856; -38.245213 -39.92845 … -39.92845 -38.24521; … ; -38.24521 -39.92845 … -39.92845 -38.24521; -36.761856 -38.24521 … -38.24521 -36.761856], Float32[305.0 305.0 … 305.0 305.0; 308.1741 308.1741 … 308.1741 308.1741; … ; 31.825891 31.825888 … 31.825888 31.825891; 35.0 35.0 … 35.0 35.0;;; 35.0 35.0 … 35.0 35.0; 38.17411 38.174114 … 38.174114 38.17411; … ; 121.82589 121.82588 … 121.82588 121.82589; 125.0 125.0 … 125.0 125.0;;; 35.0 31.825891 … 308.1741 305.0; 38.17411 35.0 … 305.0 301.8259; … ; 121.82589 125.0 … 215.0 218.17412; 125.0 128.17412 … 211.82588 215.0;;; 125.0 128.17412 … 211.82588 215.0; 125.0 128.17412 … 211.82588 215.0; … ; 125.0 128.17412 … 211.82588 215.0; 125.0 128.17412 … 211.82588 215.0;;; 215.0 218.17412 … 301.8259 305.0; 215.0 218.17412 … 301.8259 305.0; … ; 215.0 218.17412 … 301.8259 305.0; 215.0 218.17412 … 301.8259 305.0;;; 215.0 218.17412 … 301.8259 305.0; 211.82588 215.0 … 305.0 308.1741; … ; 128.17412 125.0 … 35.0 31.825891; 125.0 121.82589 … 38.17411 35.0], Float32[-35.264393 -32.3257 … 32.3257 35.264393; -36.69255 -33.698105 … 33.698105 36.69255; … ; -36.692554 -33.698105 … 33.698105 36.692554; -35.264393 -32.3257 … 32.3257 35.264393;;; -35.264393 -32.3257 … 32.3257 35.264393; -36.69255 -33.698105 … 33.698105 36.69255; … ; -36.692554 -33.698105 … 33.698105 36.692554; -35.264393 -32.3257 … 32.3257 35.264393;;; 35.264393 36.692554 … 36.69255 35.264393; 36.69255 38.313576 … 38.313576 36.692554; … ; 36.692554 38.313576 … 38.313576 36.69255; 35.264393 36.69255 … 36.692554 35.264393;;; 35.264393 36.69255 … 36.692554 35.264393; 32.3257 33.698105 … 33.698105 32.3257; … ; -32.3257 -33.698105 … -33.698105 -32.3257; -35.264393 -36.69255 … -36.692554 -35.264393;;; 35.264393 36.69255 … 36.692554 35.264393; 32.3257 33.698105 … 33.698105 32.3257; … ; -32.3257 -33.698105 … -33.698105 -32.3257; -35.264393 -36.69255 … -36.692554 -35.264393;;; -35.264393 -36.69255 … -36.692554 -35.264393; -36.692554 -38.313576 … -38.313576 -36.69255; … ; -36.69255 -38.313576 … -38.313576 -36.692554; -35.264393 -36.692554 … -36.69255 -35.264393])\n\nThe cube has Nc cells per panel edge across 6 panels. Internally it is folded into a 2D (Nc, 6·Nc) accumulator and un-folded to (Xdim, Ydim, nf) on write:\n\nprintln(\"C$(cs_spec.Nc): grid_shape = $(grid_shape(cs_spec)), centers = $(size(cs_spec.centers_lon))\")\nC24: grid_shape = (24, 144), centers = (24, 24, 6)\n\nGrid exactly as before — only the grid spec changed (sequential CPU path):\n\ngrid(config, cs_spec, time_spec, SubpixelGridding();\n     outfile = \"tropomi_sif_c24.nc\")\n\nOr from the CLI:\n\njulia --project=. bin/grid.jl l2 --config examples/tropomi_sif.toml \\\n    --gridType cs --Nc 360 --startDate 2020-07-01 --stopDate 2020-07-16 \\\n    --dDays 16 -o tropomi_sif_c360.nc\n\nThe output matches native GCHP NetCDF (dims nf/Ydim/Xdim, 2D lons/lats and corner_lons/corner_lats, coordinates = \"lons lats\"), so it opens directly in Panoply and xarray. Plot one panel on its 2D coordinates:\n\nimport xarray as xr\nds = xr.open_dataset(\"tropomi_sif_c360.nc\")\nds[\"sif_743\"].isel(time=0, nf=2).plot(x=\"lons\", y=\"lats\")\n\nSee the Cubed-Sphere Grids page for the full convention, output layout, and the bin/run_tropomi_c360.sh fan-out runner."},{"title":"Configuration Reference","page":"Configuration","location":"config/#Configuration-Reference","category":"section","text":"Data source configurations are defined in TOML files (legacy JSON is also supported). Each config file tells SatelliteGridding.jl where to find input data, which variables to grid, and what quality filters to apply."},{"title":"File Structure","page":"Configuration","location":"config/#File-Structure","category":"section","text":"filePattern = \"S5P_PAL__L2B_SIF____YYYYMMDD*.nc\"\nfolder = \"/path/to/data/\"\n\n[basic]\nlat = \"PRODUCT/latitude\"\nlon = \"PRODUCT/longitude\"\nlat_bnd = \"PRODUCT/SUPPORT_DATA/GEOLOCATIONS/latitude_bounds\"\nlon_bnd = \"PRODUCT/SUPPORT_DATA/GEOLOCATIONS/longitude_bounds\"\n\n[grid]\nsif_743 = \"PRODUCT/SIF_743\"\nsif_735 = \"PRODUCT/SIF_735\"\n\n[filter]\n\"PRODUCT/SUPPORT_DATA/GEOLOCATIONS/solar_zenith_angle\" = \"< 80\"\n\"PRODUCT/qa_value\" = \"> 0.5\""},{"title":"NetCDF storage and provenance","page":"Configuration","location":"config/#NetCDF-storage-and-provenance","category":"section","text":"Optional [output] settings control NetCDF precision, rounding, and lossless compression.\n\n[output]\ncompression = \"deflate\"  # or \"none\"\ndeflate_level = 4         # 0–9; 0 disables compression\nshuffle = false          # compare true on representative data\nfill_value = -999.0       # or nan\nprecision = \"float32\"    # or \"float64\"\nsignificant_digits_mean = 8   # L2 means; or \"none\"\nsignificant_digits_std = 6    # L2 standard deviations; or \"none\"\nsignificant_digits_center = 5 # center means and vegetation indices; or \"none\"\nchecksum = \"none\"        # or \"fletcher32\"\nstatistics = []          # optional L2 fields: \"sum_w2\", \"n_soundings\"\n# chunksizes = [256, 256, 1]  # lon, lat, time; must fit spatial dimensions\n\nDefault chunks are at most 1024 × 1024 spatial cells and one time slice. Cube chunks use one face; custom cube chunks follow [Xdim, Ydim, nf, time] order. Chunks must be smaller than 4 GiB at the selected precision. NetCDF precision applies to science variables, standard deviations, vegetation indices, and n. The science fill must be finite in that precision or NaN. It applies to empty science cells, including entirely empty time slices. [center] fill_value describes input values and [output] fill_value describes output storage. NetCDF stores zero in n for cells without observations and reserves its independent _FillValue of -1 for unwritten values. Entirely empty time slices also store n = 0. A positive n can accompany missing science data, for example in center cells below min_count or when a standard deviation overflows. When aggregating a variable, mask its weights where it is missing.\n\nUse statistics = [\"sum_w2\", \"n_soundings\"] to write either or both overlap statistics alongside n in L2 NetCDF, shared by all science variables. sum_w2 is the sum of squared fractional footprint weights, stored in the selected output precision without decimal rounding. n_soundings counts distinct contributing footprints per cell, regardless of the number of sub-samples. It is stored as Int32. Gridding and HEALPix coarsening raise an overflow error if the count exceeds 2,147,483,647. Both fields store zero for empty cells and sum over disjoint days within each rolling window. Center-coordinate and Zarr output reject these options.\n\nFor independent soundings with a common mean and variance, use the fractional overlaps as reliability weights. With M2 = n * std², compute Kish effective sample size N_eff = n² / sum_w2, unbiased sample variance s² = M2 / (n − sum_w2/n) = std² * N_eff / (N_eff − 1), and standard error SE = std / √(N_eff − 1). Require sum_w2 > 0, n_soundings ≥ 2, and N_eff > 1. Mask a variable's missing mean or std even where the shared overlap statistics are present. Avoid M2 / (n − 1) and std / √n.\n\nOverlapping rolling slices reuse soundings. A footprint shared by adjacent cells induces covariance, and correlated retrievals weaken the independence assumption. Do not use coarsened HEALPix sum_w2 or n_soundings for sample variance or SE. HEALPix coarsening.\n\nEach significant-digit setting accepts a positive integer or \"none\". Requests above 9 digits for Float32 or 17 for Float64 leave the output value unrounded. Rounding uses the output type, with the default Float32 digit settings retaining the historical rounding in grid precision. NetCDF writes the science fill when a value is non-finite or overflows during decimal rounding or conversion to the output type.\n\nTo archive Float64 results exactly, accumulate in Float64, set precision = \"float64\", and set significant_digits_mean and significant_digits_std to \"none\" for L2 or significant_digits_center to \"none\" for center mode. Set fill_value = nan to preserve finite science values that coincide with a finite fill marker. In Julia, use Float64 bounds and spacings in GridSpec (RectangularGridSpec), or pass T=Float64 to CubedSphereGridSpec or HEALPixGridSpec for sequential gridding. On the sequential CLI, --accumulator-precision float64 constructs a Float64 grid. KA CPU and CUDA accumulate in Float64 by default. Set accumulator_precision in Julia or --accumulator-precision on the CLI to choose the KA accumulator precision. Metal requires Float32. Sequential rolling runs accumulate daily moments in Float64 for both mean and standard deviation. All rolling standard deviations merge centered daily moments in Float64 before applying the output settings. Use this Float64 configuration for outputs that feed aggregation, including HEALPix 4:1 coarsening and regridding. Quantization of nearly equal child means can dominate a small parent standard deviation at fine nside. L2 NetCDF records accumulator_precision and, for spherical grids, geometry_precision as global attributes.\n\nZarr output requires precision = \"float32\" and ignores significant_digits_*. It narrows science values and standard errors to Float32 and rounds weights to Int16 counts. Its science fill is NaN and its count fill is -1 for unobserved cells. A small positive weight can round to a count of zero.\n\nNetCDF outputs always record the package version, nearest Git tag, commit, tracked dirty state, Julia version, command (when supplied), and filters. Unavailable Git fields are \"unknown\". Input satellite version fields are detected automatically, including OCO's product_version, root BuildId, and Metadata/BuildId. Optional metadata selectors control what else is retained:\n\n[metadata]\n# Optional override; first available source determines each file's product version.\n# version_sources = [\"@product_version\", \"@BuildId\", \"Metadata/BuildId\"]\nattributes = [\"@institution\", \"@identifier_product_doi\", \"Metadata/CollectionLabel\"]\nvariable_attributes = [\"units\", \"long_name\", \"description\"]\n\nUse @name for global attributes, Group/@name for group attributes, and Group/name for small metadata variables (at most 1024 elements). satellite_product_version lists distinct primary versions; source_versions and source_metadata are JSON maps retaining all distinct selected values across opened input files. Missing optional fields are skipped. Attributes describing packing or missing-value storage cannot be copied to science variables. The default variable attributes are units, long_name, valid_range, description, unit, and longname. Center-mode HDF4 skips source metadata. Additive Zarr stores keep cumulative versions and software provenance per science variable in processing_provenance."},{"title":"Top-Level Fields","page":"Configuration","location":"config/#Top-Level-Fields","category":"section","text":"Field Description\nfilePattern Glob pattern for input files. Supports date placeholders: YYYY (year), MM (month), DD (day), DOY (day of year)\nfolder Root directory for input data. Also supports YYYY/MM/DD placeholders for date-organized directory trees"},{"title":"Date Placeholders","page":"Configuration","location":"config/#Date-Placeholders","category":"section","text":"The file pattern and folder path support these placeholders:\n\nExisting directories are treated literally. For templated folders, only the path below the deepest existing parent is expanded, so names containing DD or MM by coincidence are preserved.\n\nYYYY — 4-digit year (e.g., 2019)\nYYMMDD — compact date (e.g., 190701)\nMM — 2-digit month (e.g., 07)\nDD — 2-digit day (e.g., 01)\nDOY — 3-digit day of year (e.g., 182)\n\nExample for OCO-2 with date-organized directories:\n\nfilePattern = \"oco2_LtCO2_YYMMDD_*.nc4\"\nfolder = \"/data/OCO2/B9_SIF/\""},{"title":"[basic] Section","page":"Configuration","location":"config/#[basic]-Section","category":"section","text":"Maps internal coordinate keys to NetCDF variable paths.\n\nKey Description\nlat Center latitude variable. Optional for L2 if centers can be derived from bounds\nlon Center longitude variable. Optional for L2 if centers can be derived from bounds\nlat_bnd Corner latitude bounds (N×4 or 4×N array)\nlon_bnd Corner longitude bounds (N×4 or 4×N array)\nradius Optional circular footprint radius variable for center-plus-radius products\n\nFor quadrilateral L2 gridding, lat_bnd and lon_bnd are required. If lat and lon are omitted, centers are derived from the four bound coordinates. For circular L2 gridding, either provide lat_bnd/lon_bnd or provide center lat/lon plus radius in [basic] or a scalar [circle] radius. For center-coordinate gridding (grid_center), only lat and lon are needed. For MODIS sinusoidal tiles, [basic] can be omitted and geolocation can be generated from the tile ID in the filename.\n\nNetCDF group paths use / separators (e.g., \"PRODUCT/SUPPORT_DATA/GEOLOCATIONS/latitude_bounds\")."},{"title":"[grid] Section (Required)","page":"Configuration","location":"config/#[grid]-Section-(Required)","category":"section","text":"Maps output variable names to input NetCDF variable paths. The key becomes the variable name in the output NetCDF file.\n\n[grid]\nsif_743 = \"PRODUCT/SIF_743\"           # Output name = \"sif_743\"\ncloud_fraction = \"PRODUCT/SUPPORT_DATA/INPUT_DATA/cloud_fraction_L2\"\n\nVariables are gridded in the order they appear. For MODIS vegetation indices, use explicit band-role options in [center] so the result does not depend on implicit column order."},{"title":"[center] Section (Optional)","page":"Configuration","location":"config/#[center]-Section-(Optional)","category":"section","text":"Center-coordinate options are used by grid_center:\n\nKey Description\nscale_factor Multiplicative scale applied to raw values\nadd_offset Offset added after scaling\nfill_value Raw value to reject\nvalid_min / valid_max Raw valid range\nmin_count Minimum observations required before writing a grid-cell value\nmin_nir_reflectance Optional low-NIR filter after scaling\nmodis_pixels MODIS pixels per tile edge; 2400 for 500 m MCD43A4\nvegetation_red / vegetation_nir / vegetation_blue / vegetation_swir [grid] keys used for EVI, NDVI, NIRv, and NDWI"},{"title":"[circle] Section (Optional)","page":"Configuration","location":"config/#[circle]-Section-(Optional)","category":"section","text":"Circular-footprint options are used by CircularFootprintGridding:\n\nKey Description\nradius Scalar footprint radius used for every sounding when [basic] radius is not present\nradius_unit Radius unit: degrees, km, or m. Default: degrees"},{"title":"[filter] Section (Optional)","page":"Configuration","location":"config/#[filter]-Section-(Optional)","category":"section","text":"Quality filter rules using intuitive string expressions. Each key is a NetCDF variable path, and the value is a filter expression."},{"title":"Filter Expressions","page":"Configuration","location":"config/#Filter-Expressions","category":"section","text":"Syntax Meaning Example\n\"< value\" Less than \"< 80\"\n\"> value\" Greater than \"> 0.5\"\n\"== value\" Equality \"== 0\"\n\"lo < x < hi\" Range (exclusive) \"1600 < x < 2200\"\n\nAll filters are combined with AND logic — an observation must pass all filters to be included in the gridding."},{"title":"Examples","page":"Configuration","location":"config/#Examples","category":"section","text":"[filter]\n# Solar zenith angle less than 80 degrees\n\"PRODUCT/SUPPORT_DATA/GEOLOCATIONS/solar_zenith_angle\" = \"< 80\"\n\n# Quality flag equals 0 (good quality)\n\"xco2_quality_flag\" = \"== 0\"\n\n# Methane in valid range\n\"PRODUCT/methane_mixing_ratio_bias_corrected\" = \"1600 < x < 2200\""},{"title":"Example Configurations","page":"Configuration","location":"config/#Example-Configurations","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"TROPOMI SIF","page":"Configuration","location":"config/#TROPOMI-SIF","category":"section","text":"filePattern = \"S5P_PAL__L2B_SIF____YYYYMMDD*.nc\"\nfolder = \"/data/TROPOMI/SIF/\"\n\n[basic]\nlat = \"PRODUCT/latitude\"\nlon = \"PRODUCT/longitude\"\nlat_bnd = \"PRODUCT/SUPPORT_DATA/GEOLOCATIONS/latitude_bounds\"\nlon_bnd = \"PRODUCT/SUPPORT_DATA/GEOLOCATIONS/longitude_bounds\"\n\n[grid]\nsif_743 = \"PRODUCT/SIF_743\"\nsif_735 = \"PRODUCT/SIF_735\"\n\n[filter]\n\"PRODUCT/SUPPORT_DATA/GEOLOCATIONS/solar_zenith_angle\" = \"< 80\""},{"title":"OCO-2 XCO₂","page":"Configuration","location":"config/#OCO-2-XCO","category":"section","text":"filePattern = \"oco2_LtCO2_YYMMDD_*.nc4\"\nfolder = \"/data/OCO2/B9_SIF/\"\n\n[basic]\nlat = \"latitude\"\nlon = \"longitude\"\nlat_bnd = \"vertex_latitude\"\nlon_bnd = \"vertex_longitude\"\n\n[grid]\nxco2 = \"xco2\"\naod = \"Retrieval/aod_total\"\n\n[filter]\n\"xco2_quality_flag\" = \"== 0\""},{"title":"TROPOMI CH₄","page":"Configuration","location":"config/#TROPOMI-CH","category":"section","text":"filePattern = \"S5P_RPRO_L2__CH4____YYYYMMDD*.nc\"\nfolder = \"/data/TROPOMI/CH4/\"\n\n[basic]\nlat = \"PRODUCT/latitude\"\nlon = \"PRODUCT/longitude\"\nlat_bnd = \"PRODUCT/SUPPORT_DATA/GEOLOCATIONS/latitude_bounds\"\nlon_bnd = \"PRODUCT/SUPPORT_DATA/GEOLOCATIONS/longitude_bounds\"\n\n[grid]\nmethane = \"PRODUCT/methane_mixing_ratio_bias_corrected\"\n\n[filter]\n\"PRODUCT/qa_value\" = \"> 0.5\"\n\"PRODUCT/methane_mixing_ratio_bias_corrected\" = \"1600 < x < 2200\""},{"title":"GOSAT SIF Circular Footprints","page":"Configuration","location":"config/#GOSAT-SIF-Circular-Footprints","category":"section","text":"With four circular-footprint bounding coordinates:\n\nfilePattern = \"YYYY/MM/GOSAT-SIF-L2-??MMDD-B9200-fv1.nc\"\nfolder = \"/data/GOSAT_LITE/\"\n\n[basic]\n# Center latitude/longitude are optional. If omitted, centers are derived from\n# the four footprint coordinates.\nlat = \"Latitude\"\nlon = \"Longitude\"\nlat_bnd = \"Latitude_corners\"\nlon_bnd = \"Longitude_corners\"\n\n[grid]\nsif = \"Daily_Averaged_SIF\"\nsif_757P = \"SIF_P_757nm\"\nsif_771P = \"SIF_P_771nm\"\n\n[filter]\nSZA = \"< 80\"\n\nWith center coordinates and a scalar footprint radius:\n\nfilePattern = \"YYYY/MM/GOSAT-SIF-L2-??MMDD-B9200-fv1.nc\"\nfolder = \"/data/GOSAT_LITE/\"\n\n[basic]\nlat = \"Latitude\"\nlon = \"Longitude\"\n\n[circle]\nradius = 5.25\nradius_unit = \"km\"\n\n[grid]\nsif = \"Daily_Averaged_SIF\"\nsif_757P = \"SIF_P_757nm\"\nsif_771P = \"SIF_P_771nm\"\n\n[filter]\nSZA = \"< 80\"\n\nIf radius varies by sounding, put the variable in [basic] instead:\n\n[basic]\nlat = \"Latitude\"\nlon = \"Longitude\"\nradius = \"FootprintRadius\"\n\n[circle]\nradius_unit = \"km\"\n\nUse this config with CircularFootprintGridding(...) from Julia or --footprint circle from the CLI. It supports the sequential backend and KA backends such as --backend cpu, --backend cuda, and --backend metal."},{"title":"MODIS MCD43A4","page":"Configuration","location":"config/#MODIS-MCD43A4","category":"section","text":"filePattern = \"MCD43A4.AYYYYDOY.*.hdf\"\nfolder = \"/path/to/MODIS/MCD43A4/YYYY/DOY/\"\n\n[center]\nscale_factor = 0.0001\nfill_value = 32767\nvalid_min = 0\nvalid_max = 32766\nmin_count = 5\nmin_nir_reflectance = 0.03\nmodis_pixels = 2400\nvegetation_red = \"refl_band1\"\nvegetation_nir = \"refl_band2\"\nvegetation_blue = \"refl_band3\"\nvegetation_swir = \"refl_band5\"\n\n[grid]\nrefl_band1 = \"Nadir_Reflectance_Band1\"\nrefl_band2 = \"Nadir_Reflectance_Band2\"\nrefl_band3 = \"Nadir_Reflectance_Band3\"\nrefl_band4 = \"Nadir_Reflectance_Band4\"\nrefl_band5 = \"Nadir_Reflectance_Band5\"\nrefl_band6 = \"Nadir_Reflectance_Band6\"\nrefl_band7 = \"Nadir_Reflectance_Band7\""},{"title":"Legacy JSON Format","page":"Configuration","location":"config/#Legacy-JSON-Format","category":"section","text":"The original JSON format is still supported:\n\n{\n    \"basic\": {\n        \"lat\": \"PRODUCT/latitude\",\n        \"lon\": \"PRODUCT/longitude\",\n        \"lat_bnd\": \"...\",\n        \"lon_bnd\": \"...\"\n    },\n    \"grid\": {\n        \"sif_743\": \"PRODUCT/SIF_743\"\n    },\n    \"filter_lt\": {\n        \"PRODUCT/.../solar_zenith_angle\": 80\n    },\n    \"filter_gt\": {\n        \"PRODUCT/qa_value\": 0.5\n    },\n    \"filePattern\": \"...\",\n    \"folder\": \"...\"\n}"},{"title":"GPU Acceleration","page":"GPU Acceleration","location":"gpu/#GPU-Acceleration","category":"section","text":"SatelliteGridding.jl supports accelerator execution through KernelAbstractions.jl. The same gridding kernels can run on the KA CPU backend, NVIDIA GPUs through CUDA.jl, and Apple GPUs through Metal.jl."},{"title":"Setup","page":"GPU Acceleration","location":"gpu/#Setup","category":"section","text":"CUDA.jl and Metal.jl are weak dependencies. They are loaded only when you request that backend or explicitly import the package:\n\nusing SatelliteGridding\n\ncuda_backend = resolve_backend(\"cuda\")    # requires CUDA.jl\nmetal_backend = resolve_backend(\"metal\")  # requires Metal.jl on macOS/Apple GPU\n\nYou can also use vendor packages directly:\n\nusing CUDA\nusing Metal\n\nCUDA.devices()\nMetal.devices()"},{"title":"Usage","page":"GPU Acceleration","location":"gpu/#Usage","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Julia API","page":"GPU Acceleration","location":"gpu/#Julia-API","category":"section","text":"grid_l2(config, grid_spec, time_spec;\n        backend = resolve_backend(\"cuda\"),\n        outfile = \"output.nc\")\n\ngrid_l2(config, grid_spec, time_spec;\n        backend = resolve_backend(\"metal\"),\n        outfile = \"output_metal.nc\")"},{"title":"CLI","page":"GPU Acceleration","location":"gpu/#CLI","category":"section","text":"julia --project=. bin/grid.jl l2 \\\n    --config examples/tropomi_sif.toml \\\n    --backend cuda \\\n    -o output.nc\n\njulia --project=. bin/grid.jl l2 \\\n    --config examples/tropomi_sif.toml \\\n    --backend metal \\\n    -o output_metal.nc\n\nRectangular grids support quadrilateral footprints (--footprint quad) and circular footprints (--footprint circle, CircularFootprintGridding). The circular path uses a separate index kernel that masks samples outside the inferred circle or ellipse before scatter accumulation. Both footprints support compute_std=true (--compSTD) on KA CPU, CUDA, and Metal in tiled and rolling runs.\n\nFor rectangular, cubed-sphere, and HEALPix grids, set accumulator_precision=Float32 or --accumulator-precision float32 to select single precision for weights, shifted sums, and daily moments. KA CPU and CUDA default to Float64. Metal requires Float32 and rejects an explicit Float64 setting."},{"title":"Spherical grids","page":"GPU Acceleration","location":"gpu/#Spherical-grids","category":"section","text":"HEALPix and cubed-sphere grids support quadrilateral Level-2 footprints on cpu, cuda, and metal, including standard deviations and centered rolling windows.\n\nspec = HEALPixGridSpec(nside=2048)\ngrid_l2(config, spec, time_spec; backend=resolve_backend(\"cuda\"),\n        geometry_precision=Float32, compute_std=true, outfile=\"healpix.nc\")\n\njulia --project=. bin/grid.jl l2 --config examples/tropomi_sif.toml \\\n    --gridType healpix --nside 2048 --backend cuda --geometry-precision float32 \\\n    --compSTD -o healpix.nc\n\nFor the cube, pass CubedSphereGridSpec(Nc=360) in Julia or use --gridType cs --Nc 360 on the CLI. See cubed-sphere precision for measured cell-assignment differences.\n\nSpherical geometry defaults to Float64 on CUDA and KA CPU. To trade a small number of cell-assignment differences near edges for potentially faster execution, pass geometry_precision=Float32 or --geometry-precision float32. This setting controls the adaptive sample count, unit-vector interpolation, and cell location. The host also quantizes the corner vectors to Float32 before upload and the kernel orders them in Float32. This geometry setting is independent of accumulator precision. Metal requires Float32 geometry.\n\nTo archive Float64 results exactly, accumulate in Float64 and set [output] precision = \"float64\", significant_digits_mean = \"none\", and significant_digits_std = \"none\". On the CLI, KA CPU and CUDA use Float64 accumulators by default. Sequential rolling runs also accumulate in Float64. For tiled sequential runs in Julia, use Float64 bounds and spacings in GridSpec or T=Float64 in HEALPixGridSpec and CubedSphereGridSpec. Use this configuration for HEALPix 4:1 coarsening or regridding. At fine nside, quantization of nearly equal child means can dominate a small parent standard deviation.\n\nAt nside=2048, Float32 shifts HEALPix cell edges by at most about 0.33 m. For representative OCO and TROPOMI footprints, the combined corner, interpolation, and locator displacement is about 0.2 m RMS and at most about 1.1 m, below the typical 50–500 m L2 geolocation uncertainty. A flipped subpoint can still change occupancy or shift a mean by an amount comparable to the scene standard deviation in sparsely grazed perimeter cells (n ≪ 1, where n is the sum of footprint weights). In the XCO₂ examples, cells with n ≳ 2 shifted by about 0.05 ppm or less."},{"title":"Architecture","page":"GPU Acceleration","location":"gpu/#Architecture","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Rectangular grids","page":"GPU Acceleration","location":"gpu/#Rectangular-grids","category":"section","text":"Level-2 input preparation orders quadrilateral corners on the host. The rectangular KA index kernels compute each footprint's sample indices and mark out-of-grid samples. The index kernels can mark single-cell footprints for a direct update. The quadrilateral kernel can also skip footprints that are too wide. Circular sampling counts all in-ellipse samples, including those dropped at the grid boundary.\n\nQuadrilateral scatter adds one contribution per sub-sample with weight 1/n². Circular scatter counts hits in each cell and adds one contribution per footprint and cell, dividing the hit count by the in-ellipse sample count."},{"title":"Spherical grids","page":"GPU Acceleration","location":"gpu/#Spherical-grids-2","category":"section","text":"Spherical gridding filters invalid observations and converts the four corners to Cartesian unit vectors on the host. The geometry step orders the corners in the tangent plane, selects the sample count for each footprint, and locates the bilinear subpoints directly from their normalized vectors. A private histogram holds eight cells and wide footprints are rescanned in batches of cell IDs. Each occupied cell receives one contribution per footprint. GPU kernels fuse geometry and atomic scatter. KA CPU workers count cells in parallel batches and buffer the counts for scatter in footprint order."},{"title":"Accumulation and standard deviation","page":"GPU Acceleration","location":"gpu/#Accumulation-and-standard-deviation","category":"section","text":"KA CPU scatters rectangular and spherical contributions serially in footprint order. GPU backends use atomic updates.\n\nThe first pass accumulates weights and weighted values shifted by a fixed per-variable reference. Each reference is the median of the values finite in the accumulator precision in the first nonempty batch, or zero if that variable has no finite values in the batch. Tiled runs choose a reference for each tile. Rolling runs share the first reference across days.\n\nFloat32 runs and runs that request standard deviation record the observed range of each cell in the first pass. The driver clamps the preliminary mean to these bounds and rereads each included file. Weighted linear deviations from that preliminary mean refine it. Float32 runs clamp the refined mean to the same observed range. When standard deviation is requested, the replay also accumulates squared deviations, then subtracts the squared linear residual divided by the weight. Constant cells with finite moments have zero standard deviation. A non-finite variance or variance input in accumulator precision produces fill for the standard deviation. With Float32 accumulators, including on Metal, the relative accuracy of the mean and standard deviation degrades as the number of cell contributions grows. Worst-case summation roundoff grows roughly as N × eps(Float32), where N is the number of contributions. For example, with 100,000 observations, relative mean errors of order 1e-4 can occur, and standard deviations below that relative scale can be lost. Float64 accumulators, the default on KA CPU and CUDA, avoid this Float32 precision limit. Input files should remain stable for both passes.\n\nRolling windows cache each day's weight and mean, plus the centered sum of squared deviations when requested. KA runs that replay do so once per calendar day and retain a low-order correction to the mean. Float64 stores the mean relative to the shared reference. Float32 stores the absolute refined mean so it retains the observed bounds on promotion to Float64. Centered daily moments merge using Chan's formula in Float64. Sequential rolling runs also accumulate each day in Float64.\n\nMean-only Float64 KA runs use a single pass. Their rolling windows add daily shifted sums in Float64 and restore the shared reference at finalization. With a distant reference, values with magnitude below about eps(Float64) * abs(reference - value) can round away in the first pass. The replay handles first-pass drift in Float32, including on Metal. Float32 precision can still absorb an overlap weight smaller than half an ulp of the accumulated cell weight. Very small squared deviations can underflow in Float32.\n\nA new spherical grid implements the AbstractSphericalGridSpec locator for use with both sequential and KA subdivision. Provide angular cell spacing in radians to enable adaptive sampling."},{"title":"Boundary Behavior and Geometry Precision","page":"GPU Acceleration","location":"gpu/#Boundary-Behavior-and-Geometry-Precision","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Geometry Precision and Sequential Parity","page":"GPU Acceleration","location":"gpu/#Geometry-Precision-and-Sequential-Parity","category":"section","text":"The rectangular KA paths mirror the sequential arithmetic — element types, subdivision formulas, and rounding steps — so that on KA CPU and CUDA the chosen oversampling factor n and the (footprint, sample, cell) assignments match the sequential path:\n\nFractional indices and automatic n (before the kernels): Both sequential and KA paths compute fractional grid indices directly from the input coordinates and GridSpec{T} bounds in promote_type(eltype(coords), T), and select automatic n from those indices before any backend transfer. When both the input coordinates and GridSpec are Float32, Float32 rounding (such as prevfloat(90f0) mapping to fractional row 91.0f0 on a 2° global grid) already occurs during this pre-kernel index calculation on both paths.\nQuadrilateral kernels (KA CPU and CUDA): Corner coordinates keep their input element type; edge subdivisions are widened to Float64 baseline points and connecting lines are subdivided in Float64, then rounded to the grid geometry type T before floor, matching compute_subpixels! and floor_indices!.\nCircular kernels (KA CPU and CUDA): Centers and corners are promoted internally to Float64 to compute symmetric radii and evaluate the sample loop, matching _circle_footprint_axes and accumulate_circular_footprint!. Both paths update a cell once per footprint. A single-cell footprint contributes exactly one unit of weight.\nWeights and means. Both quadrilateral paths update once per sub-sample with weight 1/n², an exact binary fraction for power-of-two n. For other values of n, the accumulated weights can round differently when the accumulator precisions differ. Sequential uses Welford's online mean and KA uses reference-shifted sums, so their means can also differ at rounding level.\nMetal exception: Metal does not support Float64. Any Float64 coordinate inputs are converted to Float32 on transfer, and both quadrilateral intermediate baseline interpolation and circular radius/sample arithmetic execute in Float32. On Metal, footprints or sub-samples right on cell boundaries may therefore be assigned to an adjacent cell relative to the sequential path."},{"title":"Out-of-Grid Sub-Samples and Global Boundaries","page":"GPU Acceleration","location":"gpu/#Out-of-Grid-Sub-Samples-and-Global-Boundaries","category":"section","text":"Drop without renormalization: On all rectangular paths (sequential and KA, quadrilateral and circular), sub-samples that fall outside 1..n_lon × 1..n_lat are dropped without renormalizing the remaining in-grid weights (1/n² for quadrilaterals, 1/inside_count for circles). Dropping without renormalization keeps regional boundary cells domain-invariant so accumulated weights (\"n\") reflect true fractional footprint overlap and a grazing edge footprint cannot bias a boundary cell's mean relative to interior footprints.\nGlobal edges (±180° and poles): On global rectangular grids, sub-samples beyond ±180° longitude or beyond ±90° latitude are dropped rather than wrapped across the antimeridian or folded across the poles.\nSpatial filtering in grid_l2: apply_filters requires all four footprint corners to lie strictly inside (lat_min, lat_max) × (lon_min, lon_max) (and longitude span < 50°), excluding edge-straddling footprints before accumulation.\nCircular footprint radius: Circular footprints infer symmetric latitude and longitude semi-axes from the farthest corner (max |corner - center| along each axis), which introduces ≲ 0.1% edge weight bias for physical GOSAT-like viewing geometry."},{"title":"Performance Considerations","page":"GPU Acceleration","location":"gpu/#Performance-Considerations","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Data Transfer","page":"GPU Acceleration","location":"gpu/#Data-Transfer","category":"section","text":"Grid accumulators are allocated on the GPU and stay there across files. Each file's input data is transferred to the GPU for processing. Input I/O and host preparation can dominate for workloads with small footprints or coarse grids."},{"title":"Memory","page":"GPU Acceleration","location":"gpu/#Memory","category":"section","text":"Rectangular GPU memory usage scales with:\n\nn_fp × n² × sizeof(Int32) × 2 for index arrays (ix, iy)\nn_fp × n_vars × sizeof(A) for values\nn_lon × n_lat × (n_vars + 1) × sizeof(A) for weights and shifted sums\nFor replay, two buffers per variable in Float64 standard deviation runs and three or four in Float32 runs (mean only or with standard deviation). Each buffer takes n_lon × n_lat × sizeof(A) bytes. Rectangular grid_l2 groups circular samples in the existing index arrays.\n\nFor a typical TROPOMI file (~500k soundings, n=10, 6 variables) in Float64:\n\nIndex arrays: ~400 MB\nValues: ~24 MB\nGrid (360×180): ~3.6 MB for the mean and ~6.2 MB extra for standard deviation\n\nThe spherical path uploads n_fp × 4 × 3 corner components in the geometry precision and n_fp × n_vars values in the accumulator precision per file. Dense accumulators dominate GPU memory for fine spherical grids. The weights and first-pass sums use n_cells × (n_vars + 1) × sizeof(A) bytes, with n_cells = 12 × nside² for HEALPix and 6 × Nc² for the cube. Float64 replay reuses the two observed-bound buffers for deviations. Float32 replay retains the two bound buffers and uses a separate buffer for linear deviations and another for squared deviations when computing standard deviation. At nside=2048 with six variables, peak dense Float64 allocations are about 2.82 GB for mean only or 7.65 GB (7.13 GiB) with standard deviation. Float32 needs about 5.03 GB for mean only or 6.24 GB with standard deviation. Per-file inputs are additional. In rolling runs with standard deviation, each cached day at that resolution uses about 7.65 GB in Float64 or 3.83 GB in Float32. A cached Float32 mean-only day stores two parts of the refined mean plus the weight (about 2.62 GB). The corresponding Float64 window buffer is about 5.23 GB for refined means or 7.65 GB with standard deviation."},{"title":"Batch Size","page":"GPU Acceleration","location":"gpu/#Batch-Size","category":"section","text":"grid_l2 uploads one whole input file at a time. For manual batching of rectangular quadrilateral footprints, accumulate_batch! processes one batch per call. For fine HEALPix grids, dense accumulators can dominate GPU memory even with small input files."},{"title":"Backend Comparison","page":"GPU Acceleration","location":"gpu/#Backend-Comparison","category":"section","text":"Aspect Sequential KA CPU KA CUDA KA Metal\nAlgorithm Welford (incremental mean) Shifted sums Shifted sums Shifted sums\nParallelism None Parallel geometry GPU-parallel GPU-parallel\nSTD support Single-pass Two-pass Two-pass Two-pass\nAtomic ops None None Backend atomics Backend atomics\nTypical hardware Any CPU Any CPU NVIDIA GPU Apple GPU"},{"title":"CLI Reference","page":"CLI Reference","location":"cli/#CLI-Reference","category":"section","text":"SatelliteGridding.jl includes a command-line interface via bin/grid.jl."},{"title":"Usage","page":"CLI Reference","location":"cli/#Usage","category":"section","text":"julia --project=. bin/grid.jl <command> [options]"},{"title":"Commands","page":"CLI Reference","location":"cli/#Commands","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"l2 — Grid Level-2 Data","page":"CLI Reference","location":"cli/#l2-—-Grid-Level-2-Data","category":"section","text":"Grid satellite Level-2 data with footprint oversampling.\n\njulia --project=. bin/grid.jl l2 [options]"},{"title":"Options","page":"CLI Reference","location":"cli/#Options","category":"section","text":"Flag Type Default Description\n--config, -c String required TOML/JSON configuration file\n--outFile, -o String gridded_output.nc Output NetCDF filename\n--latMin Float32 -90 Lower latitude bound\n--latMax Float32 90 Upper latitude bound\n--lonMin Float32 -180 Lower longitude bound\n--lonMax Float32 180 Upper longitude bound\n--dLat Float32 1.0 Latitude resolution (degrees, regular grid)\n--dLon Float32 1.0 Longitude resolution (degrees, regular grid)\n--gridType String rect Output grid: rect (lat/lon), cs (cubed sphere), or healpix (NESTED HEALPix)\n--Nc Int 0 Cubed-sphere cells per panel edge (e.g. 360 for C360); required with --gridType cs\n--nside Int 0 HEALPix resolution, a positive power of two required with --gridType healpix\n--csConvention String gmao Cubed-sphere convention: gmao (GEOS/GCHP) or equiangular\n--startDate String 2018-03-07 Start date (YYYY-MM-DD)\n--stopDate String 2018-10-31 Stop date (YYYY-MM-DD)\n--dDays Int 8 Time step in days (or months with --monthly)\n--monthly Flag false Use months instead of days for time step\n--oversample_temporal Float32 1.0 Temporal oversampling factor\n--sample_step Int 0 Rolling mean: snapshot spacing in days (>0 enables rolling mode)\n--window_days Int 0 Rolling mean: averaging half-width N in days (window = center ± N)\n--nOversample Int Automatic Use 0 or omit for automatic sampling. Use 1 to 1024 for a fixed sub-pixel factor.\n--footprint String quad Footprint geometry: quad or circle\n--compSTD Flag false Compute standard deviation\n--backend String sequential Compute backend: sequential, cpu, cuda, or metal\n--geometry-precision String Backend default Spherical KA geometry precision, float32 or float64\n--accumulator-precision String float32 for tiled sequential and Metal, float64 for sequential rolling, CUDA, and KA CPU Precision for weights, sums, and deviations. On sequential runs it also sets the grid precision when supplied. Sequential rolling accumulates daily moments in Float64 and rejects an explicit float32 request. Metal requires float32.\n--keepGoing Flag false Continue after per-file processing errors\n--noProgress Flag false Don't draw progress bars. By default they're drawn only when stderr is a terminal\n--quiet Flag false Suppress progress bars and run status messages"},{"title":"Examples","page":"CLI Reference","location":"cli/#Examples","category":"section","text":"Global TROPOMI SIF at 0.5° / 8-day composites:\n\njulia --project=. bin/grid.jl l2 \\\n    --config examples/tropomi_sif.toml \\\n    --dLat 0.5 --dLon 0.5 \\\n    --startDate 2019-01-01 --stopDate 2019-12-31 \\\n    --dDays 8 \\\n    -o tropomi_sif_2019_8day_05deg.nc\n\nRegional OCO-2 XCO₂ with KA CPU backend:\n\njulia --project=. bin/grid.jl l2 \\\n    --config examples/oco2_xco2.toml \\\n    --latMin -60 --latMax 80 \\\n    --dLat 2.0 --dLon 2.0 \\\n    --startDate 2019-01-01 --stopDate 2019-12-31 \\\n    --dDays 30 \\\n    --backend cpu \\\n    -o oco2_xco2_2019.nc\n\nMonthly composites with standard deviation:\n\njulia --project=. bin/grid.jl l2 \\\n    --config examples/tropomi_no2.toml \\\n    --dLat 0.25 --dLon 0.25 \\\n    --startDate 2019-01-01 --stopDate 2019-12-31 \\\n    --monthly --dDays 1 \\\n    --compSTD \\\n    -o tropomi_no2_2019_monthly.nc\n\nGOSAT SIF with circular footprints:\n\njulia --project=. bin/grid.jl l2 \\\n    --config examples/gosat_sif_center_radius.toml \\\n    --footprint circle --nOversample 80 \\\n    --backend cpu \\\n    --dLat 1.0 --dLon 1.0 \\\n    --startDate 2010-01-01 --stopDate 2010-12-31 \\\n    -o gosat_sif_2010_radius.nc\n\n--footprint circle works with --backend sequential, cpu, cuda, and metal. CUDA and Metal require the corresponding optional Julia GPU package and hardware support.\n\nDaily-sampled rolling mean over a centered ±7-day window (smooth time series):\n\njulia --project=. bin/grid.jl l2 \\\n    --config examples/tropomi_sif.toml \\\n    --dLat 0.5 --dLon 0.5 \\\n    --startDate 2019-01-01 --stopDate 2019-12-31 \\\n    --sample_step 1 --window_days 7 \\\n    -o tropomi_sif_2019_rolling15d.nc\n\nWith --sample_step > 0 the run switches to rolling mode: it emits one snapshot every --sample_step days, each averaging the centered window [center − window_days, center + window_days] (here a 15-day window stepped daily). Consecutive windows overlap, so the time series is smooth; --dDays and --oversample_temporal are ignored. Each calendar day is gridded only once and reused across overlapping windows (in-memory day cache), so finer sampling does not re-grid the same data. The time coordinate holds the window center, and every output also carries window_start/window_end recording the exact averaging interval (these clip naturally at the data edges). All four backends support rolling means and --compSTD on all three grids. Rolling standard deviations merge centered daily moments in Float64.\n\nTROPOMI SIF onto a GEOS/GCHP C360 cubed sphere with CUDA:\n\njulia --project=. bin/grid.jl l2 \\\n    --config examples/tropomi_sif.toml \\\n    --gridType cs --Nc 360 --backend cuda \\\n    --startDate 2020-07-01 --stopDate 2020-07-16 --dDays 16 \\\n    -o tropomi_sif_c360.nc\n\nSee Cubed-Sphere Grids for the convention, output layout, and the bin/run_tropomi_c360.sh fan-out runner.\n\nUse --gridType healpix --nside 64 for a NESTED HEALPix grid with 49,152 cells. See HEALPix Grids for an example and the NetCDF layout."},{"title":"center — Grid Center-Coordinate Data","page":"CLI Reference","location":"cli/#center-—-Grid-Center-Coordinate-Data","category":"section","text":"Grid data using center coordinates only (no footprint bounds). Suitable for MODIS-style data where each pixel maps to exactly one grid cell.\n\njulia --project=. bin/grid.jl center [options]"},{"title":"Options","page":"CLI Reference","location":"cli/#Options-2","category":"section","text":"Flag Type Default Description\n--config, -c String required TOML/JSON configuration file\n--outFile, -o String gridded_output.nc Output NetCDF filename\n--latMin Float32 -90 Lower latitude bound\n--latMax Float32 90 Upper latitude bound\n--lonMin Float32 -180 Lower longitude bound\n--lonMax Float32 180 Upper longitude bound\n--dLat Float32 0.5 Latitude resolution (degrees)\n--dLon Float32 0.5 Longitude resolution (degrees)\n--startDate String 2018-01-01 Start date (YYYY-MM-DD)\n--stopDate String 2018-12-31 Stop date (YYYY-MM-DD)\n--dDays Int 1 Time step in days (or months with --monthly)\n--monthly Flag false Use months instead of days for time step\n--geoProvider String auto Center geolocation provider: auto, variables, lut, or modis\n--geoTable String (none) Path to legacy monolithic geolocation lookup table (NetCDF)\n--geoCache String user cache Directory for generated per-tile MODIS sinusoidal geolocation\n--vegIndices Flag false Compute vegetation indices (EVI, NDVI, NIRv, NDWI)\n--keepGoing Flag false Continue after per-file processing errors\n--noProgress Flag false Don't draw progress bars. By default they're drawn only when stderr is a terminal\n--quiet Flag false Suppress progress bars and run status messages"},{"title":"Example","page":"CLI Reference","location":"cli/#Example","category":"section","text":"julia --project=. bin/grid.jl center \\\n    --config examples/modis_reflectance.toml \\\n    --dLat 0.05 --dLon 0.05 \\\n    --startDate 2019-01-01 --stopDate 2019-12-31 \\\n    --geoProvider modis \\\n    --vegIndices \\\n    -o modis_2019.nc\n\nMODIS geolocation tiles are generated on demand into the user cache. They can also be generated ahead of time:\n\njulia --project=. bin/generate_modis_geolocation.jl \\\n    --tiles h08v04,h09v04 \\\n    --cacheDir ~/.cache/SatelliteGridding/modis/sinusoidal_2400px_v1"},{"title":"coarsen — Combine NESTED HEALPix Cells","page":"CLI Reference","location":"cli/#coarsen-—-Combine-NESTED-HEALPix-Cells","category":"section","text":"julia --project=. bin/grid.jl coarsen --input fine.nc --output coarse.nc --levels 2\n\nFlag Type Description\n--input String Required input NESTED HEALPix NetCDF\n--output String Required output NetCDF path\n--levels Int Number of levels to coarsen. Defaults to one when the target is omitted.\n--nside Int Target power-of-two resolution, smaller than the input nside\n--legacy-std String error (default), by_name, or none\n\nSupply either --levels or --nside. Coarsening processes every time slice and writes unrounded Float64 moments. To prepare a fine archive on the sequential backend, pass --accumulator-precision float64 along with the output settings in the exact-archive recipe. For L2 NetCDF, request the optional overlap fields with output.statistics.\n\nTagged archives use their explicit standard deviation links. An untagged <name> and <name>_std pair raises an error by default before any output is created. Pass --legacy-std by_name for a legacy archive with complete, disjoint population standard deviation pairs, or --legacy-std none to treat all science fields as independent means. Untagged archives without pairs coarsen as means."},{"title":"Backends","page":"CLI Reference","location":"cli/#Backends","category":"section","text":"Backend Flag Description\nSequential --backend sequential Default. Uses Welford's online algorithm for mean/std. Single-threaded. Supports --compSTD.\nKA CPU --backend cpu KernelAbstractions CPU backend. Parallel sort + subpixel computation. Sum-based accumulation.\nKA CUDA --backend cuda KernelAbstractions CUDA backend. Requires CUDA.jl. All computation on GPU with atomic scatter.\nKA Metal --backend metal KernelAbstractions Metal backend. Requires Metal.jl on macOS/Apple GPU.\n\nThe KernelAbstractions backends use shifted sums and a replay pass for --compSTD on rectangular, cubed-sphere, and HEALPix grids."}]
}
